uant dominance toward deterministic convergence reasoning\n- DATA: frozen/delivered MLB estate\n- MARKET LINE: MGM only\n\nThe core distinction I’m locking in is:\n\n**EC3 gives us the methodology. EC4 gives us selected hardening ideas. Quant becomes an input/supporting lane, not the governing philosophy.**\n\nSo the new target should look more like:\n\n**Deterministic data framing → independent reasoning lanes → controlled evidence requests → deterministic convergence rules → synthesized decision → Kimi validation/gatekeeper → terminal output**\n\nNot:\n\n**Quant stack → multi-book market model → committee-on-committee convergence → another validation layer.**\n\nThe methodology upgrades we should selectively harvest from EC4 are the ones that improve **reasoning discipline**, not the ones that turn the system into a quantitative trading engine. That means things like stronger provenance, explicit data sufficiency, bounded evidence requests, deterministic convergence criteria, contradiction handling, and hard kill conditions can survive. Multi-book consensus, Quant scoring dominance, committee inflation, and blended confidence mechanics should not drive the architecture.\n\nThe key phrase for Luce/Coda going forward is:\n\n> **Recover EC3’s independent-reasoning + Kimi-gatekeeper philosophy, then harden it with deterministic convergence controls learned from EC4 without importing EC4’s Quant-centric architecture.**\n\nThat is the architecture thesis.\n\n**SESSION FOOTER**\n- STATUS: PROCEED\n- METHODOLOGY BASE: EC3\n- EC4: selective hardening only\n- QUANT: supporting input, not governing layer\n- TARGET: deterministic convergence reasoning\n- CURRENT ROUTE: P14 Luce recovery\n- IMPLEMENTATION: HOLD\n- DON INPUT NEEDED: NO", "text_chars": 1947, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "empty_text_parts": 0, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "2922f617-fda2-49a0-9823-73e06ccc95c2", "role": "assistant", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1789687706.288116, "updated_at": null, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "reasoning_recap", "part_kinds": [], "text": "", "text_chars": 0, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "empty_text_parts": 0, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "f44f7e99-d727-4cab-869e-8fb4eb484094", "role": "user", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1789687787.29, "updated_at": 1789687809.543874, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "text", "part_kinds": ["text"], "text": "", "text_chars": 0, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": null, "has_voice_metadata": false, "empty_text_parts": 1, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "061faa15-905f-433d-ae2b-794f9081e3c8", "role": "tool", "role_name": "api_tool", "created_at": 1789687788.4700866, "updated_at": null, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 0, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "tether_browsing_display", "part_kinds": [], "text": "", "text_chars": 0, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "empty_text_parts": 0, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "c8fdce66-4326-4397-b104-71669813739a", "role": "assistant", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1789687788.568935, "updated_at": 1789687809.216008, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "text", "part_kinds": ["text"], "text": "**SESSION HEADER — 2026-09-17**\n- MODE: Don + Coda\n- TRACK: EdgeCrew AI Convergence\n- BASE: EC3 methodology recovery complete\n- EC4: selective hardening only\n- OBJECTIVE: resolve load-bearing doctrine conflicts before architecture work\n- STYLE: lean / indexed / no implementation yet\n\nThis is the return we needed. EC3 is clearly the philosophical base, and Luce separated doctrine from what the old code actually did. The recovered baseline is explicit: two independent lanes, one convergence point, missing data as uncertainty, one terminal KILL, validation that does not silently rewrite the bet, and Don as the final real-money authority. fileciteturn5file0L59-L68\n\n### [D1] Gatekeeper mutation\n**RULING: VALIDATION-ONLY.**\n\nReject the old EC3 runtime behavior where Kimi adjusted the consensus score. Luce proved that this was code reality, but it directly conflicted with the recovered doctrine. fileciteturn5file0L28-L30\n\nKimi can:\n**CONFIRM / CHALLENGE / FLAG / ESCALATE / KILL**\n\nKimi cannot:\n**rewrite the fused score or silently change the pick.**\n\nThat is one of the upgrades we keep from the later methodology.\n\n### [D2] Convergence weighting\n**RULING: do not restore the old fixed 60/40 engine-vs-AI blend.**\n\nThat old operative convergence was explicitly engine-dominant, while the service-layer alternative used confidence/precision weighting. fileciteturn5file0L23-L27\n\nFor the new architecture, I would keep the EC3 principle but not freeze the implementation yet:\n\n**independent deterministic lane + independent AI reasoning lane → converge once.**\n\nWe will design the actual convergence function later. It should be deterministic and auditable, but it does not need to preserve the old 60/40 formula.\n\n### [D3] Gatekeeper vs Filter Mastermind\n**RULING: separate functions, one final authority.**\n\nThey should not both pretend to be “final.”\n\nMy architecture interpretation:\n\n**Convergence**\n→ combines/compares reasoning\n\n**Curatorial slate pass**\n→ ranks/contextualizes the slate, identifies traps/top opportunities\n\n**Kimi Gatekeeper**\n→ final validation/challenge/kill layer\n\nThen Don decides whether to bet.\n\nThe old system conflated these layers, exactly as Luce found. fileciteturn5file0L28-L30\n\n### [D4] Missing-data rule\n**RULING: STOP/DEGRADE, never neutral-5.**\n\nThis is one of the biggest corrections.\n\nOld code skipped absent variables and effectively neutralized them at 5.0. The recovered methodology explicitly says missing or insufficient data is a stop signal, not neutral information. fileciteturn5file0L31-L34\n\nSo the new system needs explicit states like:\n\n**AVAILABLE / PARTIAL / UNAVAILABLE / STALE**\n\nand the methodology decides whether the analysis:\n- proceeds,\n- proceeds degraded,\n- or stops.\n\nNo fabricated neutrality.\n\n### [D5] Peter Rules / kill behavior\n**RULING: true veto where designated KILL.**\n\nThe old implementation treated many “hard” rules as score penalties that consensus could overcome. Luce found that even the SOW admitted those rules could be outvoted. fileciteturn5file0L31-L34\n\nThat does not survive.\n\nThere can still be:\n- penalties,\n- risk flags,\n- confidence reducers,\n\nbut anything explicitly designated **KILL** is terminal.\n\n### [D6] Vocabulary\n**RULING: simplify.**\n\nWe do not need both:\n`SPLIT / CLOSE`\n\nand:\n`DIVERGENT / CONFLICT`\n\nI would standardize later around something like:\n\n**LOCK / ALIGNED / DIVERGENT / CONFLICT**\n\nbecause those describe agreement state more cleanly than “close.”\n\n### [D7] What survives from EC3\nThe best parts are already clear:\n- default = coin flip until evidence moves it;\n- persona-differentiated independent reasoning;\n- multiple independent factors must stack before a strong grade;\n- deterministic and AI lanes remain independent until convergence;\n- human authority stays outside the pipeline. fileciteturn5file0L15-L22 fileciteturn5file0L9-L11\n\nAnd this is important: **we keep the philosophy, not the monolith.** Luce explicitly found that the shipped monolith was the actual EC3 runtime while the services tree was aspirational. fileciteturn5file0L9-L10\n\nSo now we can finally move from archaeology to architecture.\n\nThe emerging target is:\n\n**Frozen MLB dataset  \n→ deterministic framing  \n→ deterministic EC3-style engine lane  \n→ independent AI persona panel  \n→ single deterministic convergence  \n→ curatorial slate pass  \n→ Kimi validation-only gatekeeper  \n→ one terminal KILL  \n→ Don decision**\n\nThat is much closer to what you have been describing.\n\n**SESSION FOOTER**\n- STATUS: PROCEED\n- EC3 RECOVERY: CLOSED\n- D1 gatekeeper mutation: resolved — validation-only\n- D2 fixed 60/40: rejected as mandatory architecture\n- D3 duplicate finals: resolved conceptually\n- D4 neutral missing data: rejected\n- D5 KILL semantics: terminal\n- D6 convergence vocabulary: simplify later\n- NEXT GATE: architecture the new EC3-derived convergence flow against the hardened frozen MLB data contract\n- IMPLEMENTATION: HOLD\n- DON INPUT NEEDED: only when we start drawing the new architecture", "text_chars": 5030, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "empty_text_parts": 0, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "4ec639b3-29fd-4a18-8d9f-1f20cef137d9", "role": "tool", "role_name": "api_tool", "created_at": 1789687788.5696998, "updated_at": null, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "multimodal_text", "part_kinds": ["text", "text"], "text": "Make sure to include fileciteturn5file0L5-L8 in your response to cite this file, or to surface it as a link. Replace the placeholder line range with the actual line range from the result that best fits the snippet you want to surface with the citation. If you cite multiple files, use a separate complete `filecite` marker for each file. Never put multiple `turnNfileM` targets inside a single `filecite` marker.\nUse ref_id \"turn5file0\" to reference this file. For broad questions or unknown locations, retrieve relevant chunks with `files.search` scoped to this file. Use `files.find` only for exact text, 