dgeCrew methodology into the smallest coherent terminal-first architecture that can run through Amazon Bedrock.\n\n### APPROVED METHODOLOGY\n\nBase = recovered EC3 philosophy, selectively hardened by EC4 lessons.\n\nRequired laws:\n\n1. Frozen/delivered MLB dataset is the authoritative data input.\n2. AI Convergence consumes the frozen estate; it does not reacquire data.\n3. MGM is the betting line used by this process.\n4. Deterministic lane and AI reasoning lanes remain independent until one convergence point.\n5. Missing/stale/insufficient data is explicit uncertainty or STOP/DEGRADE — never neutral-default scoring.\n6. Persona-differentiated AI analysts reason independently.\n7. Convergence is deterministic, auditable, and occurs exactly once.\n8. Convergence may measure/fuse agreement but may not hide model reasoning or manufacture consensus.\n9. Curatorial slate reasoning is separate from convergence.\n10. Kimi is the final validation-only gatekeeper:\n   `CONFIRM / CHALLENGE / FLAG / ESCALATE / KILL`\n11. Kimi does not mutate the score or silently rewrite the pick.\n12. Exactly one terminal KILL; KILL is final.\n13. Don remains the final human betting authority.\n14. Quant is supporting evidence/methodology only; Quant does not govern AI Convergence.\n15. DevPad/React is deferred. First runtime is CLI + artifacts + structured logs.\n16. MODEL != ROLE. Runtime occupants must remain replaceable.\n\n### TARGET FLOW\n\n`Frozen Dataset`\n→ `Binding / Framing`\n→ `Deterministic Lane`\n→ `Independent AI Analyst Panel`\n→ `Single Convergence`\n→ `Curatorial Slate Pass`\n→ `Kimi Gatekeeper`\n→ `Terminal Resolution`\n→ `Structured Output`\n→ `Don`\n\n## Architecture Questions\n\n**[A] COMPONENT BOUNDARIES**\nDefine the minimum components/services/modules required to implement the flow above.\n\nFor each:\n- responsibility\n- inputs\n- outputs\n- prohibited responsibilities\n\n**[B] DATA FLOW**\nTrace one matchup from frozen dataset through final artifact.\n\nIdentify:\n- immutable state\n- derived state\n- model-generated state\n- deterministic state\n- human-decision boundary\n\n**[C] CONTRACT BOUNDARIES**\nDefine the minimum contracts between:\n1. frozen dataset → framing adapter\n2. framing → deterministic lane\n3. framing/evidence → analyst panel\n4. analysts + deterministic lane → convergence\n5. convergence → curator\n6. curator/convergence/evidence → Kimi gatekeeper\n7. gatekeeper → terminal output\n\nDo not fully design schemas yet; define contract responsibility and required classes of fields.\n\n**[D] BEDROCK RUNTIME ARCHITECTURE**\nPropose the cleanest terminal-first Bedrock execution pattern:\n- model invocation boundary\n- model-family isolation\n- retries/timeouts\n- structured-output validation\n- failure handling\n- logging/provenance\n- model substitution without changing role semantics\n\nDo not choose specific models unless architecture requires it.\n\n**[E] STATE / ARTIFACT MODEL**\nIdentify the minimum persistent artifacts needed so every run can be replayed/audited.\n\nSeparate:\n- source dataset identity\n- framing\n- analyst responses\n- convergence result\n- curatorial result\n- gatekeeper verdict\n- terminal result\n- runtime/evidence log\n\n**[F] FAILURE / KILL SEMANTICS**\nArchitect:\n- data STOP\n- model failure\n- partial panel\n- convergence failure\n- gatekeeper KILL\n- runtime exception\n\nEnsure only one terminal KILL authority exists.\n\n**[G] ANTI-CONTAMINATION CHECK**\nIdentify any architectural pattern that would accidentally reintroduce:\n- Quant dominance\n- EC4 committee inflation\n- duplicate convergence layers\n- hidden score mutation\n- duplicate data acquisition\n- model-as-authority coupling\n- UI dependency\n\n**[H] ARCHITECTURE VERDICT**\nReturn:\n- `PASS`\n- `PASS WITH CHANGES`\n- `STOP`\n\nIf changes are required, give only the smallest architecture corrections necessary.\n\n## Constraints\n\n- No code.\n- No filesystem mutations.\n- No UI design.\n- No methodology redesign.\n- No