 basically I say copy to my keyboard. And it copies the last response to my— to my— it basically, instead of Don selecting everything to copy and paste, I say copy. To clipboard. Or just copy. And it gives Don the last response so I can paste to you. This should be a simple command in terminal for Don. ", "text_chars": 436, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": null, "has_voice_metadata": false, "empty_text_parts": 0, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "d8ea99c5-924c-435c-9934-ca9e625cfd20", "role": "assistant", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1789689907.164738, "updated_at": 1789689924.209345, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "text", "part_kinds": ["text"], "text": "Yeah, this should stay dead simple for Don: **finish a model response → type `copy` → the most recent assistant/model response is now on the Windows clipboard.**\n\nThe important part is that Talon should first recover whether we already had this working before rather than building a second mechanism.\n\n:::writing{variant=\"standard\" id=\"84173\" title=\"Talon — Restore Don Last-Response Clipboard Command\"}\n**[UTIL-COPY-1] TALON — RESTORE DON LAST-RESPONSE CLIPBOARD COMMAND**\n\n**Objective:** Give Don one terminal command:\n\n`copy`\n\nthat places the **most recent model/assistant response from the active CLI session** onto the Windows clipboard so Don can immediately paste it elsewhere.\n\nKeep this minimal. No new framework subsystem.\n\n## 1. RECON FIRST\n\nDetermine whether a prior implementation already exists in current shell/profile/tooling surfaces.\n\nSearch only likely active configuration surfaces first:\n- PowerShell profile(s)\n- active CLI helper scripts\n- current GCE tools/utilities\n- Kimi Code / OpenCode integration helpers if relevant\n\nDo not perform broad archive archaeology unless necessary.\n\nLook for prior commands/functions resembling:\n- `copy`\n- `copylast`\n- `copy-response`\n- clipboard helpers\n- `Set-Clipboard`\n- `clip.exe`\n\nIf a valid existing implementation exists, prefer restoring/rebinding it rather than creating another mechanism.\n\n## 2. REQUIRED USER EXPERIENCE\n\nFrom an active terminal session after a model responds:\n\n`copy`\n\nExpected result:\n\n- extracts only the **last completed assistant/model response**\n- excludes Don's prompt\n- excludes terminal/tool/debug noise where possible\n- copies plain text to Windows clipboard\n- returns one short confirmation such as:\n  `Copied last response to clipboard.`\n\nDon should then be able to immediately `Ctrl+V` into ChatGPT or another application.\n\nOptional alias only if trivial:\n\n`copy to clipboard`\n\nDo not require Don to specify filenames, session IDs, or paths.\n\n## 3. SCOPE\n\nInitial target:\n- current Kimi Code CLI workflow\n- current Windows / PowerShell environment\n\nIf OpenCode can use the exact same implementation safely, expose it there too. Do not fork separate implementations unnecessarily.\n\n## 4. FAILURE BEHAVIOR\n\nIf no completed assistant response can be identified:\n\n`STOP — no model response available to copy.`\n\nNever silently copy the whole terminal transcript.\n\n## 5. IMPLEMENTATION PRINCIPLE\n\nPrefer the smallest reliable mechanism.\n\nAcceptable shape:\n\n`CLI session/history source → extract latest assistant response → Set-Clipboard`\n\nNo UI.\nNo daemon.\nNo background service.\nNo framework redesign.\n\n## RETURN\n\n1. Existing prior mechanism found: YES / NO\n2. Source of latest-response text\n3. Exact command exposed to Don\n4. Files changed, if any\n5. Test:\n   - produce model response\n   - run `copy`\n   - paste result\n   - verify exact latest assistant response only\n6. `PASS / BLOCKED`\n\nSTOP after bounded implementation/test.\n:::\n\nOne design rule I’d hold firm on: **`copy` means model response, not generic Windows file-copy behavior inside our AI terminal workflow.** If Talon finds that naming collides with something important, then `copylast` is the fallback—but `copy` is the Don-friendly target.\n\nThis is exactly the kind of little ergonomic improvement we want now: remove the repetitive human copy/select/paste bullshit without adding infrastructure.", "text_chars": 3357, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "empty_text_parts": 0, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "c69f33a9-0bf3-44eb-8587-5be75fe5e793", "role": "assistant", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1789689910.214046, "updated_at": null, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "reasoning_recap", "part_kinds": [], "text": "", "text_chars": 0, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "empty_text_parts": 0, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "30f33b36-2f44-40f9-99af-833b4dd081bc", "role": "user", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1789689940.442, "updated_at": 1789689950.308549, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "text", "part_kinds": ["text"], "text": "I know we're going to get to the point where the handoffs are being read by files, but I prefer orchestrating hands-on. So this little thing, copy, it'll save Don a bunch of time and accuracy. ", "text_chars": 193, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": null, "has_voice_metadata": false, "empty_text_parts": 0, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "9071249b-a954-45a5-89c5-13e5cd96c4e3", "role": "assistant", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1789689941.706033, "updated_at": 1789689950.038319, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "text", "part_kinds": ["text"], "text": "Exactly. That’s the point.\n\nEven when we eventually have the handoffs read directly from files, **Don still stays the orchestrator**. File-based routing should remove clerical friction, not remove your control of the sequence.\n\n`copy` is valuable because it improves the current human-in-the-loop workflow immediately:\n\n**model returns → Don reviews → `copy` → paste to Coda/next seat**\n\nNo mouse-dragging through a giant terminal response, no accidentally missing the first or last paragraph, no copying terminal junk. It saves time **and** reduces handoff corruption.\n\nAnd later, when automated file handoffs are mature, the command still has value as the manual override / inspection path. So this isn't throwaway tooling.\n\nI'd keep the requirement extremely strict:\n\n> **`copy` always means: put the last completed assistant response from this active session on Don's Windows clipboard, exactly and only.**\n\nThat's a very Don-style control: tiny command, clear behavior, no intelligence required.", "text_chars": 1000, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "empty_text_parts": 0, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "eb6627c1-e461-4881-9be5-caa6a6e02032", "role": "system", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1789689944.7917666, "updated_at": null, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 0, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "text", "part_kinds": ["text"], "text": "", "text_chars": 0, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "empty_text_parts": 1, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "dcd0d6fc-2f41-43e5-9867-456dde1ddc18", "role": "system", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1789689944.7941537, "updated_at": null, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 0, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "text", "part_kinds": ["text"], "text": "", "text_chars": 0, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": 