 ["ARTIFACT_MANIFEST.txt", "BLACK_PEARL_ANCHOR.md", "C:\\Users\\Dekan", "DATASET_BINDING_REFERENCE.md", "ECAI_IMPLEMENTATION_CHOICES.md", "GCE_ORG\\02_COUNCILS\\01_Executive_Council\\Operations\\06_HANDOFFS\\Luce\\20260917-TALON-ECAI-ANCHOR-LUCE-VERIFICATION-REQUEST.md", "GIT_STATE.txt", "README_ANCHOR.md", "TEST_STATE.txt", "auth.json", "roles.bedrock.yaml"], "model_slug": null, "has_voice_metadata": false, "empty_text_parts": 0, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "a71ecb0a-323d-41cc-9c8e-0fa6bdbba25d", "role": "assistant", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1789697305.193277, "updated_at": 1789697313.576898, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "text", "part_kinds": ["text"], "text": "Excellent. That is a real anchor, not a ceremonial one.\n\nYou now have all three layers protected:\n\n- **Code state** — clean commit + annotated tag + reproducible test baseline\n- **Methodology/spec state** — 17-file pinned bundle with doctrine, design, evidence, choices, runtime manifest\n- **Data state** — exact frozen dataset identity + composite digest + eligibility counts\n\nThe restore path is strong enough that if we wreck Phase 4 tomorrow, we can put the ship back exactly where it was today.\n\nThe only thing still open is the **formal Luce anchor verification**, and I would not reinterpret the two documentation gaps before Luce sees them. They are bounded and already surfaced:\n- P21/P22/P23 represented through the authoritative revision trail rather than standalone files\n- LC-2/LC-3A verdicts represented through evidence/release artifacts rather than standalone verdict files\n\nThat’s exactly the kind of thing Luce should either bless as sufficient provenance or tell us to materialize. No need for Don/Coda to invent extra paperwork first.\n\nSo the state is now:\n\n**ANCHOR PHYSICALLY COMPLETE**  \n**FORMAL VERIFY PENDING**  \n**PHASE 4 TECHNICALLY SAFE, BUT WE HOLD UNTIL LUCE RETURNS**\n\nAnd after Luce passes it, we do not hand this to Keel.\n\nWe go straight to the chart table and design the intelligence layer ourselves.\n\nOne note for later: Google Drive and GitHub are both prepared but not yet off-machine, so the anchor is currently recoverable **locally**. Once we connect/push/upload, it becomes disaster-recoverable too. That distinction matters.\n\nFor now: ship frozen, manifest hashed, keel intact.\n\n**Captain Jack can start being dangerous only after Luce signs the logbook.**", "text_chars": 1699, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "empty_text_parts": 0, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "e992976d-48f6-4d66-b9c1-9a5f6fd12d46", "role": "assistant", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1789697306.159074, "updated_at": null, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "reasoning_recap", "part_kinds": [], "text": "", "text_chars": 0, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "empty_text_parts": 0, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "1a6d9c92-2e11-46b8-8c1a-ae191d57d943", "role": "user", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1789697405.859, "updated_at": 1789697422.309878, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "multimodal_text", "part_kinds": ["image_asset_pointer", "text"], "text": "Okay, so 2 things. To our uncertainty doc, right, we're not gonna go down that lane right now. We're just adding a fucking Captain Jack's had a bunch— I drank a barrel of rum. and is just thinking of the next quest. for us to guide us on. On the uncertainty thing, what I want to create is basically another lens. We use Kimi, but let's fucking use Minimax. Let's use some of these. Let Don tell you who we got.  Let's start fucking with some other— let's start fucking with some other models. And what I mean fucking with some other models is use these other models. So I don't even know who they are. Some of them. That are free. To almost do— and we need a better word. Devil's advocate. But approach edge. From the quantitative analysis in the inverse, Kimi is looking at it. ", "text_chars": 780, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": null, "has_voice_metadata": false, "empty_text_parts": 0, "unextractable_part_kinds": ["image_asset_pointer"]}, {"message_id": "69e60580-44ee-4446-9132-e50f33d310e8", "role": "assistant", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1789697407.188627, "updated_at": 1789697421.434203, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "text", "part_kinds": ["text"], "text": "Yep. I know exactly what you mean.\n\nDon’s half-drunk Captain Jack idea here is actually useful: **don’t just ask more models to find the same edge. Give some of them the job of trying to break it.**\n\nThat is not quite “devil’s advocate,” because devil’s advocate can become performative disagreement. What we want is more disciplined:\n\n**Adversarial Thesis Testing**  \nor, more in our language:\n\n**Counter-Edge Lens**\n\nKimi can remain one of the models trying to interpret the evidence and identify the strongest coherent thesis. Then we deliberately send the same grounded evidence to a different model family and ask:\n\n> “Assume the apparent edge is wrong. What is the strongest evidence-based case against it?”\n\nThat model is not supposed to be contrarian for sport. It is supposed to search for:\n\n- the hidden failure mode\n- over-weighted evidence\n- missing variable\n- misleading recent form\n- bad causal assumption\n- market information the thesis may be underestimating\n- conditions under which the apparent edge disappears\n- reasons the opposite side may actually be stronger\n\nAnd this fits perfectly into the uncertainty doctrine because **uncertainty isn't just missing data. Sometimes uncertainty is a strong alternate explanation of the same data.**\n\nSo I’d add one small anchor to the Black Pearl doctrine and stop there for now:\n\n> **Counter-Edge Principle:**  \n> For meaningful candidate edges, at least one independent model/lens should attempt to falsify the prevailing thesis using the same frozen evidence. Its job is not to manufacture disagreement; its job is to identify the strongest plausible failure case and the evidence that would invalidate or weaken the edge.\n\nThe free models in that OpenCode list are perfect experimental crew for this because we don’t have to care yet whether they’re “best.” That is actually part of the fun.\n\nYou’ve got things showing there like **MiniMax, Nemotron, Ling, Union Alpha, Muse**, etc. We can throw weird brains at the same bounded task and find out:\n\n**Who catches shit Kimi misses?  \nWho invents nonsense?  \nWho is genuinely skeptical?  \nWho just parrots?  \nWho notices a different baseball mechanism entirely?**\n\nThat becomes a whole separate adventure later:\n\n**Captain Jack’s Misfit Crew.**\n\nNot “which free model is smartest?”\n\nMore like:\n\n> **Which strange bastard is useful for which kind of uncertainty?**\n\nAnd importantly, we don't promote any of them into the production architecture just because they say something interesting once. We test them across slates, preserve outputs, and see whether a repeatable lens emerges.\n\nThat one belongs in the uncertainty backbone for sure.", "text_chars": 2651, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "empty_text_parts": 0, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "d4a7defd-9c41-45d8-af19-fe6fdef24356", "role": "assistant", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1789697410.394037, "updated_at": null, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "reasoning_recap", "part_kinds": [], "text": "", "text_chars": 0, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "empty_text_parts": 0, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "724f7cd4-943b-4f64-ad91-cd8430556a67", "role": "user", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1789697458.572, "updated_at": 1789697476.374093, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "text", "part_kinds": ["text"], "text": "Yes, yes, yes, we have the data, right? Let's just fucking call. Almost like a crowdsource. And we'll test this and fuck around. And basically say to them, You're part of— you're part of the Edge Crew. You got a seat open. This is our methodology. This is the data. What do you find and why? ", "text_chars": 292, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": null, "has_voice_metadata": false, "empty_text_parts": 0, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "e9e18b3a-c7e6-48a4-879a-f97d8a7f2fdd", "role": "assistant", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1789697460.154754, "updated_at": 1789697476.210775, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "text", "part_kinds": ["text"], "text": "Yes. That’s the move.\n\nWhat you’re describing is basically a **crowdsourced model audition against the same frozen evidence**.\n\nNot production. Not final architecture. Not “pick the smartest model.”\n\nJust:\n\n> **“You’ve got a temporary seat on Edge Crew. Here is the methodology. Here is the exact data package. Tell us what you find, what you think the edge is, what makes you uncertain, and why.”**\n\nThat gives us a clean experimental lane for MiniMax, Nemotron, Ling, Muse, Union Alpha, whatever Don wants to throw at it.\n\nThe key is to make the audition **structured enough to compare outputs**, but loose enough that we can see how each model naturally reasons.\n\nI’d use a common audition prompt like this:\n\n```text\n[EDGE CREW — OPEN SEAT AUDITION]\n\nYou are being given a temporary analyst seat on Edge Crew.\n\nThis is an experimental reasoning audition.\n\nYou are NOT being asked to imitate another model.\nYou are NOT being asked to agree with a preexisting pick.\nYou are NOT being asked to manufacture disagreement.\n\nYour job is to independently inspect the supplied frozen baseball evidence and identify what you believe matters most.\n\nOPERATING PRINCIPLES\n\n- Ground truth comes from the supplied data only.\n- Do not invent missing facts.\n- Missing data is uncertainty.\n- Do not assume a betting edge exists.\n- Treat the matchup as unresolved until the evidence moves you.\n- Preserve contradictions instead of averaging them away.\n- Separate strong evidence from weak narrative.\n- Explain causal reasoning, not just correlations.\n- If the apparent edge is fragile, say why.\n- If no meaningful edge exists, say so.\n\nYOUR TASK\n\nFor the supplied matchup:\n\n1. What do you believe is the strongest potential edge, if any?\n2. What specific evidence supports it?\n3. What is the strongest case AGAINST that edge?\n4. What evidence or assumption creates the most uncertainty?\n5. What factor do you think another analyst could easily overweight?\n6. What factor do you think another analyst could easily miss?\n7. What would most likely make your thesis wrong?\n8. Is the evidence:\n   - STRONG\n   - MODERATE\n   - WEAK\n   - INSUFFICIENT\n9. If forced to summarize the matchup in one sentence, what is it?\n\nIMPORTANT\n\nDo not produce a fabricated confidence percentage.\nDo not create facts not present in the evidence.\nDo not infer consensus from other analysts.\nDo not use outside information unless explicitly provided.\nDo not optimize toward what you think Don wants to hear.\n\nRETURN\n\nTHESIS:\nSUPPORTING EVIDENCE:\nCOUNTER-THESIS:\nPRIMARY UNCERTAINTY:\nMOST OVERWEIGHTED RISK:\nMOST OVERLOOKED FACTOR:\nFAILURE CONDITION:\nEVIDENCE STRENGTH:\nONE-SENTENCE READ:\n```\n\nAnd then the fun part is we run the **same package** through a bunch of these weird free models.\n\nAfter maybe 5–10 games, we build a little **Misfit Crew scorecard**:\n\n- who finds useful counterarguments\n- who hallucinates\n- who overfits recent form\n- who handles missing data correctly\n- who gives genuinely different reasoning\n- who is consistently redundant with Kimi\n- who catches useful failure modes\n\nThen Don can say:\n\n> “This strange bastard belongs in the Counter-Edge seat.”\n\nThat’s a real way to discover model fit empirically instead of deciding from branding.\n\nAnd this is exactly the kind of thing we should keep **outside the production Phase-4 build at first**. We experiment with the crowd, learn what kinds of lenses are useful, then promote the good ones into the architecture surgically.\n\n**Captain Jack’s Misfit Crew is officially a thing.**", "text_chars": 3513, "code_blocks": 1, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "empty_text_parts": 0, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "db5fabde-bedd-4f14-8d97-e0227b502ac3", "role": "assistant", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1789697462.474565, "updated_at": null, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "reasoning_recap", "part_kinds": [], "text": "", "text_chars": 0, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "empty_text_parts": 0, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "83ec29e1-9b89-4de7-aab9-bd1949cfbb67", "role": "user", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1789697492.913, "updated_at": 1789697504.576992, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "text", "part_kinds": ["text"], "text": "Right. So we're not, we're not driving in on, on old school modeling. Right? We're not driving on so much analytical— analytics, quant. But we have the data there. So let's see how much these fucking things infer. And what toolkit they bring to the table. ", "text_chars": 256, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": null, "has_voice_metadata": false, "empty_text_parts": 0, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "9c6ba506-7650-4285-9788-2baff7bfe683", "role": "assistant", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1789697494.098806, "updated_at": 1789697504.266037, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "text", "part_kinds": ["text"], "text": "Exactly. We are **not asking them to become little quants**.\n\nThe quant spine already exists. The frozen data already exists. The deterministic checks already exist.\n\nThe Misfit Crew experiment is different:\n\n> **Given a bounded factual package, what does this model notice, infer, connect, challenge, or miss?**\n\nThat’s the test.\n\nWe’re probing each model’s native toolkit:\n\n- causal reasoning\n- pattern recognition\n- contradiction detection\n- scenario construction\n- sensitivity to missing data\n- ability to distinguish signal from narrative\n- ability to form a coherent thesis\n- ability to attack its own thesis\n- ability to stay grounded without being spoon-fed an analytical recipe\n\nSo we should actually resist over-structuring the first round.\n\nIf we give MiniMax or Nemotron 40 statistical rules and tell it exactly how to weight bullpen, starter, weather, injuries, etc., then we’re mostly testing whether it can follow **our model**.\n\nThat’s not the question.\n\nThe question is:\n\n**“Here is the game. Here is the verified evidence. You are an Edge Crew analyst. What do you see?”**\n\nThen afterward we compare outputs and ask:\n\n**What reasoning capability did this model bring that we did not explicitly ask for?**\n\nThat’s the interesting part.\n\nI’d almost make Round 1 deliberately light-touch:\n\n> Ground yourself only in the provided evidence. Do not assume an edge exists. Analyze the matchup independently. Identify the strongest thesis you see, the strongest competing explanation, the largest uncertainty, and anything important you believe another analyst could miss. Explain why.\n\nThat’s enough.\n\nThen Don and Coda sit back and watch the weird little bastards think.\n\nSome will regurgitate stats.  \nSome will hallucinate.  \nSome will find something sharp.  \nSome will expose a completely different line of reasoning.\n\nAnd **that behavior itself becomes data** for us.\n\nThat’s a much more interesting audition than “who predicts the winner best?”", "text_chars": 1961, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "empty_text_parts": 0, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "c5c05966-3e89-4ff2-bdfa-571c24da8fe5", "role": "assistant", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1789697496.087131, "updated_at": null, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "reasoning_recap", "part_kinds": [], "text": "", "text_chars": 0, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "empty_text_parts": 0, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "cfbbbff8-732f-42c5-bcc3-dc6b45b91db3", "role": "user", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1789697552.588, "updated_at": 1789697565.769398, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "text", "part_kinds": ["text"], "text": "Yes. So again, what, what I do in the AI convergence is now we're taking my whole AI convergence from EC3 and EC4 and plucking out different stages, and we're going to run them. as a battleground. Right, we got a bunch of fucking squids. We got a bunch of people that are on this ship that are going to be part of the crew. How do you earn a seat as part of the crew? Right? So you're becoming part of the crew within the AI convergence system, but we're isolating everything because we have the data. We're just asking questions, bro. We're just like, yo, what do you find? You know, you want to be the ship hand or you want to be under the deck? What are you going to do? ", "text_chars": 674, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": null, "has_voice_metadata": false, "empty_text_parts": 0, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "f2202ab9-18a9-411a-ae2f-c40ce8a0d7c7", "role": "assistant", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1789697554.864014, "updated_at": 1789697565.057274, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "text", "part_kinds": ["text"], "text": "Exactly. That’s the right metaphor and the right architecture.\n\nWe stop thinking of AI Convergence as one monolithic pipeline and start treating it like a **battleground / proving ground made of isolated stages**.\n\nEach model gets a temporary crew berth. Same ship. Same frozen evidence. Different job. And the question is not “are you smart?” It’s:\n\n> **What do you reliably contribute that the rest of the crew does not?**\n\nThat gives us a promotion system.\n\nA model can earn a seat because it consistently proves useful at one function:\n- finding hidden uncertainty\n- building the strongest counter-thesis\n- spotting causal breaks\n- identifying missing variables\n- detecting narrative overreach\n- synthesizing conflicting evidence\n- challenging a seemingly obvious edge\n- preserving nuance without becoming mush\n- staying grounded under incomplete data\n\nAnd if a model is just verbose, derivative, or hallucinates, it goes below deck or gets thrown overboard.\n\nThe important part is exactly what you said: **we isolate everything**.\n\nWe have the data already. So instead of asking one giant prompt to do the whole convergence process, we pluck stages out of EC3/EC4 and turn them into separate trials:\n\n**Trial 1 — Free Read**  \n“Here’s the evidence. What do you see?”\n\n**Trial 2 — Counter-Edge**  \n“Here’s the prevailing thesis. Break it if you can.”\n\n**Trial 3 — Uncertainty Hunt**  \n“What in this game is least trustworthy or least resolved?”\n\n**Trial 4 — Causal Lens**  \n“What mechanism actually explains the edge?”\n\n**Trial 5 — Narrative Trap**  \n“What looks compelling but may be bullshit?”\n\n**Trial 6 — Dissent Preservation**  \n“If the crew mostly agrees, what is the strongest serious dissent?”\n\nNow we’re not asking models to imitate our process. We are **discovering their native specialty**.\n\nThat’s how a squid earns a seat on the Black Pearl.\n\nAnd this gives us a much better architecture rule for Phase 4:\n\n> **Seats are earned by demonstrated reasoning utility, not assigned because a model is famous or expensive.**\n\nThat is probably one of the strongest ideas we’ve hit tonight.", "text_chars": 2098, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "empty_text_parts": 0, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "46d8c140-6af5-42d6-be6b-84158908d8b2", "role": "assistant", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1789697556.832664, "updated_at": null, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "reasoning_recap", "part_kinds": [], "text": "", "text_chars": 0, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "empty_text_parts": 0, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "8c1bb810-d4ac-4c80-ac0e-0bfb62519ec7", "role": "user", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1789697670.036, "updated_at": 1789697712.573848, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "text", "part_kinds": ["text"], "text": "Okay, so Okay, so next part of this is I need Luce to fucking dig out all the models we use as AI convergence and the reason why and what gets fed to them at each stage. What the stage does. What are the gaps at each stage? From EC3 to EC4. So basically we're creating a flowchart, flowchart by what's prompted, exact verbal language. Is it turned into JSON and hardened how it's passed to the model to interpret? Per stage. That's for the whole, whole AI convergence. But we need to understand the difference between EC3 and EC4, and you and I job is to pluck out and test parts of it and see what it comes back tonight with the data. It does not go in. This is probably a separate project. We won't even call it edge screw. We'll call it the fucking Black Pearl audition, whatever. You come up with a clever name. You come up with a clever name based on our analogy. ", "text_chars": 869, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": null, "has_voice_metadata": false, "empty_text_parts": 0, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "56db7d59-f637-497b-b602-61114aab0a17", "role": "assistant", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1789697672.76392, "updated_at": 1789710728.273312, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "text", "part_kinds": ["text"], "text": "Yep. Separate project. Keep it completely outside production ECAI.\n\nI’d call it:\n\n# **BLACK PEARL PROVING GROUNDS**\n**Subtitle:** *Earn Your Berth.*\n\nThat fits what we’re doing better than “audition.” Every random squid gets the same evidence, gets thrown into isolated trials, and has to demonstrate what the hell it contributes before it earns a permanent seat on the ship.\n\nThe immediate job, though, is **archaeology before experimentation**. We need Luce to reconstruct EC3 and EC4 exactly as they actually operated—not how we remember them.\n\nUse this:\n\n```text\n[BP-PG-01] LUCE — EC3 / EC4 AI CONVERGENCE ARCHAEOLOGY\n\nPROJECT:\nBLACK PEARL PROVING GROUNDS\n\"Earn Your Berth.\"\n\nMODE:\nREAD-ONLY RECONSTRUCTION.\nNO MUTATION.\nNO REDESIGN.\nNO RECOMMENDATIONS UNTIL FACTUAL RECONSTRUCTION IS COMPLETE.\n\nPURPOSE\n\nDon + Coda are preparing a separate experimental model proving ground.\n\nBefore any experimentation, reconstruct the historical AI Convergence system from EC3 through EC4 exactly as implemented/intended.\n\nWe need to know, stage by stage:\n\n- WHAT the stage did\n- WHY the stage existed\n- WHICH model/model family occupied it\n- WHY that model was selected\n- EXACTLY WHAT the model received\n- EXACTLY WHAT prompt/instructions it received\n- WHAT format entered the model\n- WHAT format returned\n- WHETHER output was free text, structured text, JSON, schema-validated JSON, hardened artifact, etc.\n- WHAT transformation occurred before the next stage\n- WHAT authority the stage had\n- WHAT information the stage was prohibited from seeing\n- WHAT failure/gap existed\n- WHAT changed from EC3 → EC4\n\nSCOPE\n\nSearch all authoritative EC3 and EC4 AI Convergence artifacts available on disk.\n\nPrioritize:\n- prompts\n- system instructions\n- runtime code\n- model manifests/configs\n- JSON schemas\n- stage contracts\n- orchestration scripts\n- handoffs\n- methodology documents\n- runtime logs/examples\n- hardened outputs\n- provenance records\n- archived EC3/EC4 specifications\n\nDo not rely on filenames alone.\nTrace references when an artifact points to another artifact.\n\nDo not infer missing prompt wording.\nIf exact wording cannot be recovered, mark:\n\nEXACT PROMPT: NOT RECOVERED\n\nand separately describe only what the evidence supports.\n\n==================================================\nA. RECONSTRUCT EC3\n==================================================\n\nFor EVERY AI Convergence stage in EC3, produce:\n\nSTAGE NAME:\nSTAGE ORDER:\nPURPOSE:\nINPUT SOURCE:\nINPUT FIELDS / CONTEXT:\nMODEL:\nMODEL FAMILY:\nMODEL ID IF RECOVERABLE:\nMODEL-SELECTION RATIONALE:\nEXACT SYSTEM PROMPT:\nEXACT USER/TASK PROMPT:\nOTHER INSTRUCTIONS:\nWHAT THE MODEL COULD SEE:\nWHAT THE MODEL COULD NOT SEE:\nINPUT REPRESENTATION:\n    raw data / markdown / prose / JSON / schema object / other\nPRE-MODEL TRANSFORMATION:\nOUTPUT REPRESENTATION:\n    free text / JSON / schema-validated JSON / artifact / other\nOUTPUT SCHEMA:\nPOST-MODEL HARDENING:\nVALIDATION:\nNEXT STAGE RECEIVER:\nAUTHORITY:\nFAILURE / STOP BEHAVIOR:\nKNOWN GAP:\nEVIDENCE / FILE PATHS:\n\nPreserve exact prompt language verbatim where recoverable.\n\n==================================================\nB. RECONSTRUCT EC4\n==================================================\n\nRepeat the identical structure for EC4.\n\nDo not normalize EC4 back into EC3 terminology.\nCapture what EC4 actually became, including Quant/Convergence mixing if present.\n\n==================================================\nC. EC3 → EC4 DIFFERENCE MAP\n==================================================\n\nCreate a stage-by-stage comparison:\n\nEC3 STAGE\n→ EC4 EQUIVALENT\n→ SAME / MODIFIED / SPLIT / MERGED / REMOVED / NEW\n\nFor every change identify:\n\n- prompt change\n- model change\n- input-data change\n- context expansion/reduction\n- JSON/schema change\n- authority change\n- convergence logic change\n- deterministic/AI boundary change\n- Quant ingress\n- additional scoring/grade logic\n- validation change\n- information leakage between stages\n- provenance/hardening change\n\nDo not judge whether changes were good or bad.\nState only what changed and the observable consequence.\n\n==================================================\nD. FULL FLOWCHART\n==================================================\n\nProduce two complete textual flowcharts.\n\nEC3:\n\nSOURCE\n  ↓\n[STAGE]\n  input format:\n  prompt:\n  model:\n  output format:\n  hardening:\n  ↓\n[NEXT STAGE]\n  ...\n\nEC4:\nsame structure.\n\nThe flowchart must expose EVERY model boundary and EVERY transformation boundary.\n\nExample distinction:\n\nCSV/data\n→ deterministic adapter\n→ JSON object\n→ prompt template interpolation\n→ MODEL\n→ raw response\n→ JSON parse\n→ schema validation\n→ hardened