l → Haiku / Qwen\n- market → GPT-4o / Mistral\n- narrative → Sonnet / Gemini\n- historical → DeepSeek / Kimi\n\nEach committee was told **grade one game on your specific lens only**, not to make the final decision, and to return structured JSON with grade, score, reasoning, and concerns. fileciteturn13file0L156-L205\n\nThat is fucking valuable.\n\nNot because we should rebuild EC4. We shouldn't.\n\nBut because **that is exactly the kind of component we can pull out and throw into the Black Pearl Proving Grounds.**\n\nThe home/away lens is another separate animal. Those models received:\n- the game matrix,\n- committee outputs,\n- coaching history,\nand then produced a bounded signed bump with confidence. fileciteturn13file0L209-L269\n\nThen EC4 fusion went even further: multiple judge models deliberated over the surviving slate and aggregated LOCK/STRONG/LEAN/PASS outcomes. fileciteturn13file0L287-L294\n\nAnd the gatekeeper changed character completely. EC3’s Kimi gatekeeper was explicitly told it could **CONFIRM / CHALLENGE / BOOST** and its adjustment mutated consensus. fileciteturn13file0L83-L112 EC4’s later gatekeeper was framed as the final validation layer looking for what everybody missed, with broader visibility over model votes and convergence results. fileciteturn13file0L297-L313\n\nThat gives Don and Coda the exact excavation map we wanted.\n\n### Where I want us to go next\n\nWe do **not** immediately pick a final new architecture.\n\nWe build a **chart-table stage board** from this archaeology with one row per historical reasoning component:\n\n| Historical component | What it was trying to do | What it saw | Model(s) | Prompt philosophy | What was interesting |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| EC3 crowdsource | broad independent read | nearly full game packet | 10 MLB models | same task, JSON answer | raw model diversity |\n| EC4 statistical committee | statistical lens | matrix, exclusions | GLM pair | lens-isolated | specialization |\n| EC4 situational committee | contextual factors | matrix, exclusions | Haiku/Qwen | lens-isolated | non-statistical reasoning |\n| EC4 market committee | price/market lens | market-specific | GPT/Mistral | lens-isolated | market interpretation |\n| EC4 narrative committee | injuries/context | constrained | Sonnet/Gemini | anti-hype | qualitative reasoning |\n| EC4 historical committee | historical context | constrained | DeepSeek/Kimi | lens-isolated | pattern/history |\n| EC4 home/away lens | asymmetric context | committee outputs + matrix | Fable/DeepSeek | signed bump | post-panel interpretation |\n| EC4 fusion | deliberation | surviving slate | 3 judges | compare/synthesize | cross-thesis reasoning |\n| gatekeeper | attack final case | broad downstream view | Kimi/Claude variants | validation | adversarial review |\n\nThen for **Black Pearl Proving Grounds**, we pull individual rows out and ask:\n\n> What happens if we give this exact historical job to MiniMax?  \n> What happens if GLM does it?  \n> What happens if Kimi does it?  \n> What happens if Nemotron does it?\n\nSame frozen data. Same prompt. Same output contract.\n\nThat is the fucking battleground.\n\nAnd now we can finally do this scientifically because the archaeology also exposed the old weaknesses:\n- EC3 panel wasn’t truly isolated;\n- EC4 blindness lived partly in prompt text instead of hard code;\n- provenance per model was weak;\n- model rosters drifted;\n- gatekeeper authority changed between generations;\n- EC4 even had multiple competing pipeline definitions alive at once. fileciteturn13file0L480-L510\n\nSo I would make our very next Don/Coda exercise:\n\n**BLACK PEARL TRIAL #1 — EC4 BLIND COMMITTEE**\n\nTake one game from the frozen dataset.\n\nGive **exactly the same isolated lens prompt** to 4–6 different models.\n\nNo DET.\nNo other analysts.\nNo convergence.\nNo pick history.\nNo outcome knowledge.\n\nThen compare:\n\n**what they noticed, what they inferred, what they missed, what they fabricated, and what uncertainty each one exposed.**\n\nThat would be our first real crew audition.\n\nWe have the map now, Captain. Now we get to start stealing the good pieces.", "text_chars": 5632, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "empty_text_parts": 0, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "06be00c2-d122-442d-b13b-a77ca9252423", "role": "tool", "role_name": "api_tool", "created_at": 1789713946.1356647, "updated_at": null, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "multimodal_text", "part_kinds": ["text", "text"], "text": "Make sure to include fileciteturn13file0L5-L8 in your response to cite this file, or to surface it as a link. Replace the placeholder line range with the actual line range from the result that best fits the snippet you want to surface with the citation. If you cite multiple files, use a separate complete `filecite` marker for each file. Never put multiple `turnNfileM` targets inside a single `filecite` marker.\nUse ref_id \"turn13file0\" to reference this file. For broad questions or unknown locations, retrieve relevant chunks with `files.search` scoped to this file. Use `files.find` only for exact text, and use `files.read` for a known range or to expand relevant results. Full file size: 600 file lines.\n[L1] # BLACK PEARL PROVING GROUNDS — EC3 / EC4 AI CONVERGENCE ARCHAEOLOGY\n\n[L3] - **Session:** `20260917-LUCE-BPPG01`\n[L4] - **Seat:** Luce (verification / ground truth). Occupant/model: Big Pickle (`MODEL != SEAT`).\n[L5] - **Mode:** READ-ONLY reconstruction of the recovered evidence. No redesign. No recommendations. No mutation of any source artifact. No Black Pearl model has been touched, built, or tested.\n[L6] - **Tagging:** `[GIVEN]` = stated verbatim in a recovered artifact · `[DERIVED]` = computed/inferred from recovered artifacts with the derivation shown · `[PROPOSED]` 