hat I like to do is separate them into market presence. Let's have 2 because you cheated already. If I could pick GLM too, I would pick GLM. You see my goal here and intent. This is actually an overarching thing I wanted to do anyway. ", "text_chars": 511, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": null, "has_voice_metadata": false, "empty_text_parts": 0, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "f40f8627-d612-4906-b6fe-d6515bada0ac", "role": "assistant", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1789719592.676307, "updated_at": 1789719608.514216, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "text", "part_kinds": ["text"], "text": "Yes. That’s cleaner than “big tech vs little tech.”\n\nWhat you actually care about is **market presence / ecosystem gravity**, because that tells us whether we’re just rediscovering obvious incumbents or whether we’re finding useful capability from models most people ignore.\n\nSo let’s split the draft board into two sheets:\n\n### Sheet A — High Market Presence\nModels/families with strong awareness, distribution, enterprise adoption, or broad ecosystem visibility.\n\nExamples:\n- OpenAI\n- Anthropic\n- Google / Gemini\n- Meta / Llama\n- DeepSeek\n- Qwen\n- Mistral\n- maybe NVIDIA/Nemotron depending on how Luce verifies current reach\n\nThese are the **known ships in the harbor**.\n\n### Sheet B — Lower / Emerging Market Presence\nModels that may be capable but have less mainstream visibility, less US mindshare, narrower distribution, newer branding, or more limited ecosystem penetration.\n\nExamples from your screenshots:\n- GLM\n- MiniMax / MiMo\n- Muse Spark\n- Ling\n- Nex\n- Laguna\n- Inkling\n- Dots\n- North\n- LFM\n- some Nemotron variants depending on how we define presence\n\nThese are the **strange ships coming out of the fog**.\n\nAnd yes, this fixes my cheating problem. 😄\n\nIf we put GLM on the same board as everybody else, both Don and Coda immediately draft GLM because we already know it. That defeats part of the experiment.\n\nSo the real Black Pearl exercise becomes:\n\n> **Pick horses separately from the known-market fleet and the under-the-radar fleet.**\n\nThat way we’re forced to discover something.\n\nI’d make both sheets use the same columns:\n\n| Model | Provider | Family | Market Presence | Known/Unknown to Don | Likely Strength | Pirate Role Hypothesis | Trial 01? | Result | Earned Berth? |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n\nAnd one more column I think matters:\n\n**“Why is this model interesting despite its market presence?”**\n\nBecause that is the whole point of the exercise.\n\nA model with huge presence may be valuable because it is consistently strong.\n\nA model with almost no mindshare may be valuable because it sees something nobody else does.\n\nAnd this absolutely is an overarching thing, beyond sports:\n\n**Don + Coda model scouting should not default to brand prestige.**\n\nWe should maintain an empirical roster of:\n\n- proven incumbents\n- emerging challengers\n- weird specialists\n- cheap workhorses\n- reasoning monsters\n- critics\n- synthesis models\n- coding models\n- verification models\n\nIn pirate terms:\n\n**not every ship in the fleet needs to be a man-o’-war. Some are scouts, some are raiders, some carry powder, and some are weird little bastards that somehow outrun everybody.**\n\nThat’s the roster system we actually want.", "text_chars": 2650, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "empty_text_parts": 0, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "51ae0183-68dd-4d1d-a33a-1b4d76e3ca38", "role": "assistant", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1789719597.011197, "updated_at": null, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "reasoning_recap", "part_kinds": [], "text": "", "text_chars": 0, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "cita