text_chars": 1081, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": ["mnt/data/BLACK_PEARL_FREE_MODEL_DRAFT_BOARD_2026-09-18.xlsx"], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "empty_text_parts": 0, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "3a8c46fc-5f53-44d3-8161-49993a477f41", "role": "user", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1789720248.432, "updated_at": 1789720271.979303, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "text", "part_kinds": ["text"], "text": "Okay, I want to have fucking fun, so I can't be looking. That's good to be organized. All right. All right. We're just gonna run this. I have, I have basically no horse in the race. Basically, I like MiniMax. I don't see my boy Owl Alpha on that list. I've gotten good things out of Ling. GLM is good if we prompt it right. I haven't really messed around with the NVIDIA model as much as I want. Mistral does a good job. It's like the grounding layer. the big boy committee. I hate Ollama and Qwen That being said, Quen does a good job on certain things. I've never gotten anything good out of Ollama. By the way. Cohear whatever is a nut job. Swimming pool or whatever, Mr. Pool. He can be used too. I'm gonna stop analyzing and have some fun. Show Don the board again of like the top 5 per each split. It's easier for Don to just look at them here. Sorry, buddy. ", "text_chars": 865, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": null, "has_voice_metadata": false, "empty_text_parts": 0, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "14fb0393-b09b-4f7e-8d6f-7558a657562d", "role": "assistant", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1789720252.443113, "updated_at": 1789720271.608181, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "text", "part_kinds": ["text"], "text": "Yep. Keep it fun. Here’s the **Captain Jack quick-look board** — no spreadsheet archaeology required.\n\n### Known Fleet / Higher Market Presence\n\n| Rank | Horse | Why it’s interesting for Black Pearl |\n|---|---|---|\n| **1** | **DeepSeek V4 Flash 0731** | Fast, capable generalist. I want to see whether it actually attacks a thesis or just summarizes it. |\n| **2** | **Qwen3.8 27B** | Strong independent family. You don’t love Qwen, which honestly makes it a better blind test. If it earns a berth, it earns it. |\n| **3** | **Nemotron 3 Ultra** | NVIDIA flagship. Big open model, long context, agentic pedigree. We haven’t really pushed NVIDIA through our sports reasoning yet. |\n| **4** | **Nemotron 3 Super** | Smaller than Ultra, potentially more balanced. Great sibling test: does the “middle” model reason cleaner than the giant? |\n| **5** | **Gemma 4 31B** | Dense Google-family generalist. Not my first instinct, which is exactly why it belongs in the race. |\n\n### Ships in the Fog / Emerging-Lower Presence\n\n| Rank | Horse | Why it’s interesting for Black Pearl |\n|---|---|---|\n| **1** | **MiMo V2.5** | Your MiniMax/Xiaomi-style wild-card lane. Big enough to surprise us, unfamiliar enough to actually teach us something. |\n| **2** | **Inkling** | Flagship open generalist with calibrated-confidence claims. This one smells like a potentially very good uncertainty model. |\n| **3** | **Muse Spark 1.3** | Weird long-context/sub-agent behavior. Could find something none of the obvious models notice. |\n| **4** | **Nex-N2.5-Pro** | Agentic and autonomous. Sports reasoning is not its advertised specialty, which makes it a perfect “prove yourself” squid. |\n| **5** | **Ling 3.0 Flash Fin** | You’ve already gotten good things from Ling. Finance-tuned, so I’m curious whether it sees price/value relationships differently without becoming another Quant lane. |\n\n### Captain’s wild cards\n\n**GLM 5.2** — obviously dangerous because both Don and Coda already like it. We should probably hold it out of the *first* race specifically so we don’t bias ourselves toward the known horse.\n\n**Mistral** — I agree with your characterization: good grounding / committee-type brain. It wasn’t on this free-model board, but it absolutely belongs in the later **heavy guns** round.\n\n**Poolside / Laguna S 2.1** — Mr. Swimming Pool. Coding-first, so not an obvious baseball analyst. Which means Captain Jack absolutely gets to throw it into one weird trial later.\n\n**Cohere / North** — your “nut job” lane. North Mini Code is coding-oriented, so not Trial 01, but I’m not throwing the family overboard yet.\n\nAnd one correction: **your boy Union Alpha Free was visible in the original OpenCode screenshot, but it did not appear in Luce’s handbook**, so it never made the consolidated board. We should put **Union Alpha** on the **unverified wild-card list** and let Luce truth it separately.\n\nIf I were Don right now and wanted pure fun, I’d run:\n\n**DeepSeek V4 Flash  \nNemotron 3 Ultra  \nMiMo V2.5  \nInkling  \nMuse Spark 1.3  \nUnion Alpha as the mystery squid**\n\nNo GLM yet.\n\nMake GLM watch from the captain’s quarters while the rookies fight for a berth.", "text_chars": 3145, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "empty_text_parts": 0, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "dfb50a1b-d6b0-45ef-8d83-e1e6180af5eb", "role": "assistant", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1789720255.959893, "updated_at": null, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "reasoning_recap", "part_kinds": [], "text": "", "text_chars": 0, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "empty_text_parts": 0, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "29fef95f-92a9-4fe6-959b-ed2a5410be01", "role": "user", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1789720360.737, "updated_at": 1789720375.047567, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "text", "part_kinds": ["text"], "text": "All right, here we go. Let Don get ready. All right, we're going out the gate with someone crazy and stupid. Dot 3-Note Preview. Open router. ", "text_chars": 142, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": null, "has_voice_metadata": false, "empty_text_parts": 0, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "a57ade79-2763-4864-963f-869bdf5bafeb", "role": "assistant", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1789720364.795213, "updated_at": 1789720374.628271, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "text", "part_kinds": ["text"], "text": "Perfect. **Dots3-Note Preview gets the first berth audition.**\n\nThat’s exactly the spirit of this whole thing: don’t start with the obvious horse, start with the weird bastard and see what happens.\n\nUse the same immutable package:\n\n- `BLACK_PEARL_TRIAL01_SPARSE_SLATE_2026-09-18.json`\n- `BLACK_PEARL_TRIAL01_PROMPT.txt`\n\nNo changes to the prompt. No model-specific coaching. No hints. No Don prior. No Quant. No EC3.\n\nFor the log, I’d label it:\n\n**BP-T01-DOTS3-NOTE-PREVIEW**\n\nAnd save the raw response exactly as returned before we discuss it.\n\nOur only job after it comes back is to look at:\n- what it noticed\n- whether it stayed grounded\n- what it invented\n- what it treated as uncertainty\n- whether it found an interesting game for a non-obvious reason\n- whether its counter-thesis was real or performative\n- whether it deserves another trial\n\nCaptain Jack has officially handed the first sword to the strange guy on the dock.\n\nRun it.", "text_chars": 939, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": ["BLACK_PEARL_TRIAL01_PROMPT.txt", "BLACK_PEARL_TRIAL01_SPARSE_SLATE_2026-09-18.json"], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "empty_text_parts": 0, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "60f5918d-1ead-4c7c-ad4f-e07a47006c35", "role": "assistant", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1789720369.519818, "updated_at": null, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "reasoning_recap", "part_kinds": [], "text": "", "text_chars": 0, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "empty_text_parts": 0, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "2b299c9e-27c6-4ee8-b8ea-b4c480721cd4", "role": "user", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1789720560.111, "updated_at": 1789720659.197533, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "text", "part_kinds": ["text"], "text": "I'm going to start running a few of these at once. So I could have some fun. Okay, wow. All right, we got to update our prompt. We got to tell them to make a file on my desktop, or actually make a file here. By their name. We basically got to put in a prompt who they are, identify who they are. \n\n\"C:\\Users\\Dekan AI Brother\\Desktop\\Black Pearl EC\\OpenCode Free Test Run\"\n\nI've read the full slate. Here's my blind deckhand read.\nSelected Games (5)\n1. Kansas City Royals @ Pittsburgh Pirates\nINITIAL READ: Dobnak (R, ERA 2.73, WHIP 1.27, K9 6.10) vs. Skenes (R, ERA 3.78, WHIP 1.13, K9 10.28). Pirates -190, Royals +155. Total 8 (O -105 / U -115).\nWHY IT CAUGHT MY ATTENTION: The Pirates are heavy favorites (-190) despite their starter's ERA (3.78) being nearly a full run worse than the underdog's starter (2.73). The market treats Skenes like an anchor, but on the surface stat line Dobnak is the better pitcher.\nPOTENTIAL THESIS: Dobnak's 2.73 ERA is real and Skenes' 3.78 is misleading; the Royals at +155 are value. The market overweights Skenes' K9/whip and underweights Dobnak's ERA.\nSTRONGEST COUNTER-THESIS: Skenes' 10.28 K9 and 1.13 WHIP are better talent indicators; Dobnak's 6.10 K9 and 1.27 WHIP suggest his ERA is luck-driven. The -190 may be fair