g the Phillies are actually the stronger side; the -1.5 at +165 is mispriced, and the missing Phillies starter may be a known good pitcher the market is quietly pricing in.\nSTRONGEST COUNTER-THESIS: The run line is a data error or reflects a sharp liability position; Thornton is a known lefty at home and the Phillies' missing starter could be an unknown or bullpen game — the +100 moneyline may be correct.\nBIGGEST UNKNOWN: The identity and quality of the Phillies' starting pitcher. Entirely NOT_SET. No thesis can be confirmed without it.\nWHAT YOU WOULD WANT NEXT: The Phillies' announced starter (name, status, handedness, season stats).\nCLASSIFICATION: NEEDS DATA\n4. Washington Nationals @ St. Louis Cardinals\nINITIAL READ: Cavalli (R, ERA 3.12, WHIP 1.17, K9 10.60) vs. Leahy (R, ERA 3.33, WHIP 1.31, K9 8.53). Moneyline: Nationals -105, Cardinals -115. Run line: Nationals -1.5 @ +165, Cardinals +1.5 @ -200. Total 7.5.\nWHY IT CAUGHT MY ATTENTION: The Nationals have the better starter (lower ERA, lower WHIP, higher K9) and are the moneyline favorite despite being the road team. The run line price is generous: a -105 favorite giving 1.5 runs at +165 is unusual — the market is offering attractive odds on the Nationals to win by margin.\nPOTENTIAL THESIS: Cavalli is the better pitcher and the Nationals are the correct side; the +165 run line is value because the market underprices their ability to win by 2+.\nSTRONGEST COUNTER-THESIS: The Cardinals are at home in a near coin-flip (-115); the Nationals' road form may lag their starter stats, and the +165 may compensate for that risk.\nBIGGEST UNKNOWN: Road vs. home context for the Nationals, and the Cardinals' offensive threat against Cavalli.\nWHAT YOU WOULD WANT NEXT: Cardinals' offensive form and confirmation Cavalli is fully built up (PROBABLE only).\nCLASSIFICATION: INTEREST\n5. Chicago Cubs @ Cincinnati Reds\nINITIAL READ: Holmes (R, ERA 2.85, WHIP 1.15, K9 6.97) vs. Burns (R, ERA 2.80, WHIP 1.15, K9 10.76). Mone