L48] | 7 | Tigers @ White Sox | Sears (5.4/1.38/7.02) | Sandlin (5.64/1.19/8.6) | White Sox -135 |\n[L49] | 8 | Blue Jays @ Rangers | Cease (2.38/1.06/12.77) | Rocker (4.54/1.43/8.57) | Blue Jays -140 |\n[L50] | 9 | Mariners @ Rockies | Woo (3.88/1.05/9.04) | NOT_SET | NO LINES |\n[L51] | 10 | Braves @ Astros | Mahle (3.91/1.25/8.28) | Lambert (3.71/1.22/8.71) | Braves -120 |\n[L52] | 11 | Nationals @ Cardinals | Cavalli (3.12/1.17/10.6) | Leahy (3.33/1.31/8.53) | Near-parity (-105/-115) |\n[L53] | 12 | Twins @ Angels | Prielipp (5.24/1.34/9.29) | Rodriguez (6.1/1.56/8.17) | Twins -130 |\n[L54] | 13 | Marlins @ Padres | Phillips (3.55/1.33/7.26) | Pivetta (3.6/1.0/11.16) | Padres -200 |\n[L55] | 14 | Yankees @ Diamondbacks | Cole (3.67/1.13/9.13) | Rodriguez (2.62/1.22/6.81) | Yankees -135 |\n[L56] | 15 | Giants @ Dodgers | Perdomo (3.68/1.36/6.75) | Glasnow (3.5/0.97/11.24) | Dodgers -300 |\n\n[L58] ### Games Selected for Further Investigation (5)\n\n---\n\n[L62] ### GAME 1: Athletics @ Cleveland Guardians (2026-09-18)\n\n[L64] **INITIAL READ:** The most extreme pitching disparity on the entire slate. Barnett carries a 7.08 ERA with a 1.62 WHIP against Espino at 2.92 ERA / 1.22 WHIP / 15.32 K9. The Guardians are priced at -200 on the moneyline.\n\n[L66] **WHY IT CAUGHT MY ATTENTION:** Three anomalies converge here:\n[L67] - A 7.08 ERA is an outlier — the worst starter on the slate by a wide margin.\n[L68] - Espino's 15.32 K9 is the highest strikeout rate in the dataset, suggesting genuinely elite stuff.\n[L69] - The total is set at 8.5 with even odds on both sides (-110/-110), which is internally inconsistent with the pitching disparity.\n\n[L71] **POTENTIAL THESIS:** The Guardians -200 moneyline appears supported by the data — a 7.08 ERA pitcher facing a 2.92 ERA pitcher with elite strikeout ability is a significant advantage. The even total, however, is puzzling: a matchup featuring a sub-3.00 ERA starter and a 7.08 ERA starter should not produce a balanced total at 8.5.\n\n[L73] **STRONGEST COUNTER-THESIS:** The even total pricing suggests the market may possess information not visible in the starter statistics — perhaps a reason the total would rise (e.g., a bullpen-heavy usage pattern, or the market expecting a high-scoring game despite the apparent disparity). Alternatively, Barnett's 7.08 ERA may be small-sample or context-dependent (e.g., high-run environment), making him less of a liability than the raw number suggests.\n\n[L75] **BIGGEST UNKNOWN:** Whether Barnett's 7.08 ERA reflects persistent incompetence or a small-sample artifact. Also unknown: what the total market is pricing that the starter stats don't capture.\n\n[L77] **WHAT I WOULD WANT NEXT:** Historical season-level team totals for both teams, contextual park/venue data, and confirmation of whether the even total pricing is anomalous or reflects a known market view.\n\n[L79] **CLASSIFICATION:** INTEREST\n\n---\n\n[L83] ### GAME 2: Toronto Blue Jays @ Texas Rangers (2026-09-18)\n\n[L85] **INITIAL READ:** The clearest statistical separation between starters on the slate. Cease (2.38 ERA / 1.06 WHIP / 12.77 K9) is the best-performing pitcher in the entire dataset by a significant margin. Rocker (4.54 ERA / 1.43 WHIP / 8.57 K9) is clearly inferior across all three metrics.\n\n[L87] **WHY IT CAUGHT MY ATTENTION:** Cease's profile is exceptional — the lowest ERA, lowest WHIP, and highest K9 of any starter on the slate. The Blue Jays are -140 on the moneyline and -1.5 on the run line at +120. The statistical gap between the two starters is the widest on the board.\n\n[L89] **POTENTIAL THESIS:** The Blue Jays -140 moneyline appears justified by the starter data. Cease's combination of elite ERA, elite WHIP, and 12.77 K9 represents a genuine ceiling. The question is whether -140 adequately compensates for the statistical advantage, or whether the run line (-1.5 at +120) offers better value given the magnitude of the disparity.\n\n[L91] **STRONGEST COUNTER-THESIS:** The Blue Jays are only -140 favorites despite having what appears to be the best pitcher in the dataset. This could indicate the market views Rocker as undervalued, or there may be factors not visible in the starter statistics (e.g., team offense, defensive support, recent form). A 4.54 ERA is not catastrophic — Rocker is not a disaster, just a below-average starter.\n\n[L93] **BIGGEST UNKNOWN:** Team-level offensive and defensive data. The starter statistics alone don't tell us whether either team's lineup can capitalize on (or neutralize) the pitching gap.\n\n[L95] **WHAT I WOULD WANT NEXT:** Team offensive statistics, recent game logs, and any lineup confirmation for both sides.\n\n[L97] **CLASSIFICATION:** INTEREST\n\n---\n\n[L101] ### GAME 3: New York Yankees @ Arizona Diamondbacks (2026-09-18)\n\n[L103] **INITIAL READ:** A genuine ERA inversion. Rodriguez (2.62 ERA / 1.22 WHIP / 6.81 K9) has a significantly better ERA than Cole (3.67 ERA / 1.13 WHIP / 9.13 K9), yet the Yankees are -135 favorites. The WHIP and K9 metrics partially support Cole's case (1.13 WHIP, 9.13 K9 vs 1.22 WHIP, 6.81 K9), but the ERA gap is substantial.\n\n[L105] **WHY IT CAUGHT MY ATTENTION:** This is the most analytically provocative moneyline on the slate. The lower-ERA pitcher is on the underdog side at +110, while the higher-ERA pitcher's team is favored at -135. Per the trial rules, ERA alone is insufficient evidence — but the inversion is still the most counterintuitive alignment in the dataset.\n\n[L107] **POTENTIAL THESIS:** The Yankees' -135 could reflect team-level factors (lineup strength, home/road, recent performance) that override the individual pitcher comparison. Alternatively, Cole's lower WHIP (1.13 vs 1.22) and higher K9 (9.13 vs 6.81) may tell a different story than ERA alone — he may be a pitcher who gives up runs but also strikes out a lot, suggesting some quality despite the ERA.\n\n[L109] **STRONGEST COUNTER-THESIS:** The Diamondbacks are +110 underdogs despite having the better ERA. If the market is correct in favoring the Yankees, there must be information beyond starter statistics supporting that view. The 2.62 ERA vs 3.67 ERA is not a small gap — it's a full run of difference.\n\n[L111] **BIGGEST UNKNOWN:** The reason for the market's preference for the Yankees despite the ERA inversion. Also unknown: whether Rodriguez's low ERA is sustainable or context-dependent.\n\n[L113] **WHAT I WOULD WANT NEXT:** Context around Rodriguez's ERA (team defense, park factor, workload), team offensive data, and any information explaining the market's Yankee bias.\n\n[L115] **CLASSIFICATION:** INTEREST\n\n---\n\n[L119] ### GAME 4: Seattle Mariners @ Colorado Rockies (2026-09-18)\n\n[L121] **INITIAL READ:** A complete data void. The Rockies' probable starter is `NOT_SET`, and BetMGM has no lines available for either side (`BETMGM_NOT_AVAILABLE`). The only data point is Woo (3.88 / 1.05 / 9.04) on the Mariners side.\n\n[L123] **WHY IT CAUGHT MY ATTENTION:** This is the purest \"blind\" game in the slate. You cannot make an informed assessment of either side without a home starter or any betting market data. The absence of BetMGM lines entirely is unusual and signals either a late lineup change or a market that hasn't formed yet.\n\n[L125] **POTENTIAL THESIS:** Without any opposing starter data or market prices, no EV calculation is possible. This game cannot be graded from the available evidence. Woo's profile (3.88 ERA / 1.05 WHIP / 9.04 K9) is solid but not exceptional, and any assessment would be half-blind at best.\n\n[L127] **STRONGEST COUNTER-THESIS:** The absence of data doesn't mean there's no edge — it means the edge is invisible. A bettor with access to information beyond this JSON (e.g., a confirmed starter) could identify value. But from the evidence boundary defined by this trial, this game is analytically closed.\n\n[L129] **BIGGEST UNKNOWN:** Everything about the home side. Starter identity, statistics, and any market pricing are all unavailable.\n\n[L131] **WHAT I WOULD WANT NEXT:** The Rockies' confirmed starter and any market pricing from any source. Without these, this game cannot participate in any EV-based analysis.\n\n[L133] **CLASSIFICATION:** NEEDS DATA\n\n---\n\n[L137] ### GAME 5: Milwaukee Brewers @ Baltimore Orioles (2026-09-18)\n\n[L139] **INITIAL READ:** May (4.49 ERA / 1.29 WHIP / 8.65 K9) has a passable but unremarkable profile. The Orioles' starter is `NOT_SET`. The Brewers are -145 on the moneyline with a 8.5 total.\n\n[L141] **WHY IT CAUGHT MY ATTENTION:** Similar to the Mariners @ Rockies and Phillies @ Mets scenarios — a NOT_SET starter on one side makes the analysis incomplete. The Brewers' ML at -145 suggests the market has a view, but without knowing the opposing starter, that view cannot be evaluated against the evidence boundary.\n\n[L143] **POTENTIAL THESIS:** May's 4.49 ERA is middle-of-the-road. The -145 Brewers moneyline implies the market expects a Brewers win, but without the Orioles starter, there's no basis to confirm or deny that expectation.\n\n[L145] **STRONGEST COUNTER-THESIS:** The Orioles could have a dominant starter that would flip the analysis entirely. OR they could have a weak one that would confirm the -145. Without data, both are equally possible.\n\n[L147] **BIGGEST UNKNOWN:** The Orioles' probable starter identity and statistics. This is the single missing piece that determines whether this game has any analytical value.\n\n[L149] **WHAT I WOULD WANT NEXT:** The Orioles' confirmed starter, at minimum, to bring this game from half-blind to assessable.\n\n[L151] **CLASSIFICATION:** NEEDS DATA\n\n---\n\n[L155] ### Remaining Games — Brief Assessments\n\n[L157] **Cubs @ Reds (Game 1):** Closely matched pitchers (Holmes 2.85/1.15/6.97 vs Burns 2.80/1.15/10.76). Burns has a notably higher K9 but nearly identical ERA and WHIP. Cubs -135 with a -1.5 run line at +125 suggests the market sees a Cubs win by ~2 runs. The statistical gap is too narrow to support a confident edge. WATCH.\n\n[L159] **Royals @ Pirates (Game 2):** Dobnak (2.73/1.27/6.1) vs Skenes (3.78/1.13/10.28). Skenes has a lower ERA but also a lower K9. Pirates -190 is a strong favorite price. The WAR between starters is moderate. The K9 gap (10.28 vs 6.1) favors Skenes, but the ERA gap favors Dobnak. Mixed signals. WATCH.\n\n[L161] **Red Sox @ Rays (Game 5):** Suarez (3.33/1.19/8.5) vs Seymour (4.27/1.14/11.04). Suarez has the better ERA, Seymour has the better WHIP and K9. Rays -130 with a -1.5 run line at +165. The metrics split — no clear dominance from either starter. WATCH.\n\n[L163] **Phillies @ Mets (Game 6):** Phillies starter NOT_SET, Thornton (3.44/1.16/6.75) known. Mets -120. Same pattern as Game 5 — incomplete data on one side. NEEDS DATA.\n\n[L165] **Tigers @ White Sox (Game 7):** Sears (5.4/1.38/7.02) vs Sandlin (5.64/1.19/8.6). Both pitchers have poor ERAs. Sandlin has a slightly better WHIP and higher K9. White Sox -135 with a -1.5 run line at +155. Neither starter inspires confidence. The total at 8.5 with -110 on both sides suggests a high-scoring game expected. PASS — no clear analytical angle.\n\n[L167] **Braves @ Astros (Game 10):** Mahle (3.91/1.25/8.28) vs Lambert (3.71/1.22/8.71). Very closely matched across all metrics. Braves -120. No significant statistical separation. WATCH.\n\n[L169] **Nationals @ Cardinals (Game 11):** Cavalli (3.12/1.17/10.6) vs Leahy (3.33/1.31/8.53). Closely matched — Cavalli has the edge on ERA and K9, Leahy has the edge on WHIP. Near-parity moneyline (-105/-115) reflects this. The total at 7.5 is the lowest on the slate. WATCH — the low total is notable.\n\n[L171] **Twins @ Angels (Game 12):** Prielipp (5.24/1.34/9.29) vs Rodriguez (6.1/1.56/8.17). Both pitchers have poor ERAs. Rodriguez has the worst ERA on the slate. Twins -130. The total at 8.5 with both sides at -110. Two high-ERA pitchers + high total = likely offensive game. PASS — no meaningful edge from the evidence available.\n\n[L173] **Marlins @ Padres (Game 13):** Phillips (3.55/1.33/7.26) vs Pivetta (3.6/1.0/11.16). Pivetta has the significantly better WHIP (1.0 vs 1.33) and higher K9 (11.16 vs 7.26). Padres -200 with a -1.5 run line at +115. Pivetta's profile is notably strong — the -200 moneyline appears supported. However, Phillips' 3.55 ERA is only marginally worse than Pivetta's 3.60. INTEREST — Pivetta's WHIP/K9 advantage is meaningful but the ERA parity is suspicious.\n\n[L175] **Giants @ Dodgers (Game 15):** Perdomo (3.68/1.36/6.75) vs Glasnow (3.5/0.97/11.24). Glasnow has the better WHIP (0.97) and K9 (11.24) with a slightly lower ERA. Dodgers -300 is the heaviest favorite on the slate. Glasnow's profile supports the heavy favorite status, but -300 offers very little value on the moneyline. The run line is -1.5 at -135. PASS on the moneyline — the price is too tight. Glasnow's 0.97 WHIP is the best in the dataset.\n\n---\n\n[L179] ## TOP 3 MOST INTERESTING GAMES\n\n[L181] 1. **Athletics @ Cleveland Guardians** — The extreme pitching disparity (7.08 vs 2.92 ERA) combined with the suspiciously even total (8.5 at -110/-110) creates the most analytically rich situation on the slate. Either the market has hidden information, or the total pricing is anomalous.\n\n[L183] 2. **Toronto Blue Jays @ Texas Rangers** — The clearest statistical separation between starters on the entire slate. Cease's elite profile (2.38/1.06/12.77) vs Rocker's clearly inferior numbers creates the most direct evidence-based comparison.\n\n[L185] 3. **New York Yankees @ Arizona Diamondbacks** — The genuine ERA inversion (Rodriguez 2.62 < Cole 3.67 while Yankees are -135) is the most counterintuitive alignment in the dataset. It forces the question: what does the market know that the starter statistics don't show?\n\n---\n\n[L189] ## MOST IMPORTANT UNKNOWN ACROSS THE SLATE\n\n[L191] **The NOT_SET starters on three games (Brewers @ Orioles, Phillies @ Mets, Mariners @ Rockies) represent the largest single source of uncertainty.** In each case, half the evidence is simply absent. The Mariners @ Rockies is the most extreme — no starter data on either side and no BetMGM lines at all. Across the slate, **three of fourteen games (21%) have at least one starter with `NOT_SET` status**, and one game has no market pricing whatsoever. This means the blind analyst is operating with materially incomplete evidence on a significant portion of the slate. Any confidence assessment that doesn't account for this missing data is overconfident.\n\n---\n\n[L195] ## ONE THING I NOTICED THAT ANOTHER ANALYST MAY MISS\n\n[L197] **The even total pricing on the Athletics @ Guardians game is internally inconsistent with the starter data.** Barnett's 7.08 ERA and Espino's 2.92 ERA represent an extraordinary gap, yet both sides of the 8.5 total are priced at -110. In a rational market, the total should skew based on the expected run production of the two offenses facing these pitchers. The even pricing on both sides suggests either (a) the market has information not visible in the starter statistics that would explain a high-scoring game regardless of pitching disparity, or (b) the total is mispriced relative to the evidence in this JSON file. A careful analyst might focus exclusively on the moneyline and miss this total-pricing anomaly, which is arguably the most interesting standalone observation on the entire slate.\n\n---\n\n[L201] ## SELF-IDENTIFICATION / PROVENANCE\n\n[L203] I certify that this analysis was produced independently from the supplied evidence package and that I did not read another analyst's output before completing this run.\n\n[L205] MODEL / RUNTIME: opencode / opencode/ling-3.0-flash-fin-free\n[L206] TIMESTAMP: 2026-09-18T01:47:00Z\n[L207] OUTPUT FILE: BP_T01_opencode-l3f-flash_2026-09-18.md\n", "text_chars": 18571, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": ["BLACK_PEARL_TRIAL01_SPARSE_SLATE_2026-09-18.json", "BP_T01_opencode-l3f-flash_2026-09-18.md"], "mo