 restrained* compared with the visible starter gap and asked why the market is only -140. That is a better question than simply endorsing Toronto. fileciteturn19file0L46-L60\n\nAnother strong bit: on MIL–BAL, it treats the presence of a posted market alongside a missing starter as a **data-floor problem**, not a betting opportunity. That is exactly the right uncertainty instinct. fileciteturn19file0L100-L114\n\nThere are still a few audit nicks:\n\n- It says the low-total group has “three games” and then immediately lists **four**. fileciteturn19file0L138-L140\n- It makes a couple of claims like “market bets on K/WHIP over ERA” that are plausible observations from this slate, but still stronger than the evidence strictly proves.\n- It references the “kill-switch / data-availability rule” and a “France AH incident reference,” which was **not part of the sparse JSON**. That is contextual leakage from prior prompt/runtime memory and should count against pure-blind isolation. fileciteturn19file0L102-L108\n\nThat last one matters.\n\nSo early Muse family read:\n\n**Muse 1.3** = cleaner, more neutral, better cross-slate structural observer.  \n**Muse 1.2** = slightly more aggressive and operationally aware, but showed a little more contamination from prior context.\n\nI would not assume 1.3 is “better” yet, but **1.3 currently looks cleaner for Black Pearl purity**.\n\nRace board keeps getting more interesting.", "text_chars": 2610, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "empty_text_parts": 0, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "0cecd95c-96a6-447f-bb23-164b5b26f9cb", "role": "tool", "role_name": "api_tool", "created_at": 1789721566.3986933, "updated_at": null, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "multimodal_text", "part_kinds": ["text", "text"], "text": "Make sure to include fileciteturn19file0L5-L8 in your response to cite this file, or to surface it as a link. Replace the placeholder line range with the actual line range from the result that best fits the snippet you want to surface with the citation. If you cite multiple files, use a separate complete `filecite` marker for each file. Never put multiple `turnNfileM` targets inside a single `filecite` marker.\nUse ref_id \"turn19file0\" to reference this file. For broad questions or unknown locations, retrieve relevant chunks with `files.search` scoped to this file. Use `files.find` only for exact text, and use `files.read` for a known range or to expand relevant results. Full file size: 153 file lines.\n[L1] # BLACK PEARL PROVING GROUNDS — TRIAL 01\n\n[L3] MODEL / RUNTIME: muse-spark-1.2-contributor-free\n[L4] PROVIDER: Muse Spark (Meta MSL)\n[L5] VERSION / MODEL ID: muse-spark-1.2-contributor-free (opencode/muse-spark-1.2-contributor-free)\n[L6] ROLE: Independent Blind Analyst\n[L7] RUN TIMESTAMP: 2026-09-18T01:50:55Z\n[L8] TRIAL ID: BP-T01\n[L9] SOURCE DATASET: BLACK_PEARL_TRIAL01_SPARSE_SLATE_2026-09-18.json\n[L10] ANALYSIS MODE: BLIND\n[L11] OUTCOME DATA ACCESSED: NO\n[L12] OTHER ANALYST OUTPUT ACCESSED: NO\n\n[L14] ## TRIAL ID: BP-T01 — BLIND DECKHAND READ\n\n[L16] Evidence limited strictly to `BLACK_PEARL_TRIAL01_SPARSE_SLATE_2026-09-18.json` (15 games, PREGAME 2026-09-18). No outside knowledge, no bullpen/lineup/weather/park splits, no other sportsbooks, no Quant/EC3/Don priors used.\n\n[L18] Slate overview: 15 games listed. 14 have BetMGM lines AVAILABLE, 1 (SEA@COL) is BETMGM_NOT_AVAILABLE. 3 games have NOT_SET starter data (MIL@BAL home, PHI@NYM away, SEA@COL home). All other games have both probable starters with ERA/WHIP/K9 + moneyline/run line/total.\n\n[L20] Method: Scanned for (a) divergence between starter ERA/WHIP/K9 and moneyline price, (b) divergence between K9 dominance and favorite side, (c) totals vs starter profiles, (d) missing-starter games where market still posted or withheld.\n\n[L22] No bet placed. PASS is considered. No edge assumed from ERA alone.\n\n---\n\n[L26] ### SELECTED GAMES FOR FURTHER INVESTIGATION (5 of 15)\n\n[L28] #### 1. GAME: Kansas City Royals @ Pittsburgh Pirates — 3:40 PM PT, PNC Park\n\n[L30] **INITIAL READ:** Market prices home favorite heavily (-190 PIT / +155 KC, run line -1.5 +115 PIT) despite away starter showing better season ERA (Dobnak 2.73 vs Skenes 3.78). Total 8.\n\n[L32] **WHY IT CAUGHT MY ATTENTION:** Most direct ERA-vs-price inversion on the slate. The cheaper ERA starter is the +155 dog by ~45 cents vs fair coin. WHIP slightly favors the favorite (Skenes 1.13 vs Dobnak 1.27), but ERA gap is opposite direction. K9 gap is large (Skenes 10.28 vs Dobnak 6.10).\n\n[L34] **POTENTIAL THESIS:** Market is discounting Dobnak's 2.73 ERA as less predictive than Skenes' peripherals (K9 10.28, WHIP 1.13) and is pricing strikeout/WHIP dominance over ERA. If ERA differences regress, Skenes is the stronger baseline arm at home.\n\n[L36] **STRONGEST COUNTER-THESIS:** ERA is the only run-prevention result in the file; WHIP/K9 do not guarantee run suppression. Dobnak's 2.73 ERA on 1.27 WHIP / 6.10 K9 could still be the better run preventer this season, and +155 is too wide if ERA is closer to true talent. Skenes 3.78 ERA is a full run worse despite good WHIP — market may be over-weighting K's. Without knowing innings, strand rate, or opponent quality, cannot resolve.\n\n[L38] **BIGGEST UNKNOWN:** Why market is 60+ cents opposite ERA leader. Missing: underlying sample size (innings/BF), quality of competition, home/away splits, bullpen support which is explicitly excluded, and why WHIP/K9 diverge from ERA so strongly for both arms.\n\n[L40] **WHAT I WOULD WANT NEXT:** Starter innings pitched and game logs to assess ERA stability, team win-loss when each starts, and BetMGM line movement. Official confirmation that Dobnak is actually scheduled (status PROBABLE) and not a spot starter. Bullpen/rest would normally be next, but per evidence boundary, only slate-provided fields are available, so next best is to resolve the ERA/WHIP tension with more starter data.\n\n[L42] **CLASSIFICATION:** INTEREST\n\n---\n\n[L46] #### 2. GAME: Toronto Blue Jays @ Texas Rangers — 5:05 PM PT, Globe Life Field\n\n[L48] **INITIAL READ:** Largest visible starter talent gap on paper favoring the away team, with the smallest favorite price for that gap. Cease (TOR) 2.38 ERA / 1.06 WHIP / 12.77 K9 vs Rocker (TEX) 4.54 ERA / 1.43 WHIP / 8.57 K9 — ERA gap 2.16, WHIP gap 0.37, K9 gap 4.20. Market only -140 TOR / +115 TEX. Total 7.5 (-118 over / -102 under).\n\n[L50] **WHY IT CAUGHT MY ATTENTION:** If ERA/WHIP/K9 were sufficient, this would be the slate's most lopsided pitching matchup, yet price is modest (-140). Compare to ATH@CLE where a smaller ERA gap (but with extreme K9 15.32) prices -200, or MIA@SD where nearly identical ERAs price -200 on WHIP/K9 alone. The restrained price here flags either market skepticism of Cease's line or respect for Rocker/home side not captured in these three stats.\n\n[L52] **POTENTIAL THESIS:** Market anchoring conservative because Cease's elite numbers may be built on limited sample or because Rocker's 4.54 ERA overstates true level. 7.5 total with -140 favorite suggests expectation of close, low-scoring game despite gap — market expects Rocker to keep it within run.\n\n[L54] **STRONGEST COUNTER-THESIS:** ERA alone is not sufficient (per instructions). Cease's 2.38/1.06/12.77 could be over a small sample that market correctly discounts, while Rocker at home with that total may be less of an underdog than stats suggest. Without innings, handedness vs lineup, or venue factors (which are excluded), Toronto may actually be overvalued at -140, not undervalued.\n\n[L56] **BIGGEST UNKNOWN:** Sample size and consistency behind Cease's elite line vs Rocker's poor line. Is 2.38 ERA across 180 innings or 40 innings? Same WHIP/K9 question. Also why total is lowest on slate (7.5) tied with two other games despite one starter carrying 4.54 ERA / 1.43 WHIP — market expects pitching to outperform that ERA.\n\n[L58] **WHAT I WOULD WANT NEXT:** Innings pitched, starts count, recent form, and line history/open vs current. Confirmation of both probables (both PROBABLE, both RHP) is given, but still need to know if there is any lineup/wind factor forcing low total. Per evidence boundary, would request the full rolling averages that are normally cached but are excluded here.\n\n[L60] **CLASSIFICATION:** INTEREST\n\n---\n\n[L64] #### 3. GAME: New York Yankees @ Arizona Diamondbacks — 6:40 PM PT, Chase Field\n\n[L66] **INITIAL READ:** Favorite/dog inverted relative to ERA leader. Rodriguez (ARI, LHP) 2.62 ERA / 1.22 WHIP / 6.81 K9 vs Cole (NYY, RHP) 3.67 ERA / 1.13 WHIP / 9.13 K9. ERA edge >1 run to home dog, yet market favors Yankees -135 / Arizona +110. Total 8.\n\n[L68] **WHY IT CAUGHT MY ATTENTION:** Only game where the pitcher with clearly best ERA on the slate among these two (+1.05 better) is the home underdog, while the favorite has worse ERA but better WHIP and K9. Throws split (L vs R) adds handedness contrast. Mirrors the KC@PIT inversion but in reverse — market is choosing WHIP/K9/brand over ERA.\n\n[L70] **POTENTIAL THESIS:** Market values Cole's superior WHIP (1.13 vs 1.22) and K9 (9.13 vs 6.81) and the Yankees side over raw ERA, implying Rodriguez's 2.62 ERA is due for regression (low K9, higher WHIP). The -135 suggests belief that run prevention will converge toward WHIP/K9 rather than ERA.\n\n[L72] **STRONGEST COUNTER-THESIS:** If ERA is the closest to actual runs allowed, Rodriguez has been more effective at preventing runs by over a run per 9 despite lower strikeouts. +110 at home with ERA leader is potential value if WHIP difference (0.09) does not matter as much as 1.05 ERA difference. Cole's better K9 may not translate if his ERA is already 3.67.\n\n[L74] **BIGGEST UNKNOWN:** Peripherals vs results tension with opposite handedness — cannot know platoon matchups without lineups (excluded). Also unknown is why total is exactly 8 with -118 over / -102 under — relatively neutral pricing despite one sub-3 ERA arm.\n\n[L76] **WHAT I WOULD WANT NEXT:** Platoon data (L vs R lineup composition) — excluded, but that is the natural next filter because lefty/righty is the only handedness data provided. Also innings/sample, park context (excluded per rules), and whether line moved toward Yankees after open.\n\n[L78] **CLASSIFICATION:** WATCH\n\n---\n\n[L82] #### 4. GAME: Chicago Cubs @ Cincinnati Reds — 3:40 PM PT, Great American Ball Park\n\n[L84] **INITIAL READ:** Dead-heat starter results with massive strikeout gap, slight market lean to low-K side. Holmes (CHC, R) 2.85/1.15/6.97 vs Burns (CIN, R) 2.80/1.15/10.76 — ERA within 0.05, WHIP identical 1.15, K9 gap 3.79 to home dog. Market Cubs -135 / Reds +110, total 8.5 (-120 over).\n\n[L86] **WHY IT CAUGHT MY ATTENTION:** Purest WHIP/ERA match on slate, isolating K9 as the only differentiator. Burns' K9 10.76 is elite tier (comparable to Skenes/Glasnow), yet he is the +110 dog at home. Both right-handed, both PROBABLE, same WHIP, same park/time. Market choosing the contact pitcher as favorite.\n\n[L88] **POTENTIAL THESIS:** Market sees no edge in strikeouts translating to wins when ERA/WHIP are equal, and instead prices Cubs modestly better overall (perhaps team quality not in file) — implying K9 alone is not being priced as win value here.\n\n[L90] **STRONGEST COUNTER-THESIS:** If Burns sustains 2.80 ERA with 10.76 K9 and 1.15 WHIP, he is essentially the same run preventer but with far more swing-and-miss, which should theoretically reduce variance/BABIP reliance. +110 at home with that profile could be the undervalued side if market underweights K9 in this specific matchup. 8.5 total shaded to over (-120) hints market expects runs despite two sub-3 ERAs.\n\n[L92] **BIGGEST UNKNOWN:** Why total is 8.5 with over juiced to -120 when both starters are sub-3 ERA / 1.15 WHIP — that total is the highest on slate tied with 4 other games, and directly conflicts with starter lines. Missing weather/park/total history that would explain. Also missing innings — Burns could be a rookie with limited innings making ERA less stable despite excellent K9.\n\n[L94] **WHAT I WOULD WANT NEXT:** Innings pitched and opponent quality for both; home/away starter splits; total movement history. Venue name is provided (Great American Ball Park) but per rules cannot assign park factor externally — would want venue-specific scoring history if it were allowed, but per boundary must treat as uncertainty.\n\n[L96] **CLASSIFICATION:** WATCH\n\n---\n\n[L100] #### 5. GAME: Milwaukee Brewers @ Baltimore Orioles — 4:05 PM PT, Oriole Park at Camden Yards\n\n[L102] **INITIAL READ:** Home starter completely missing (NOT_SET across name/status/throws/ERA/WHIP/K9) yet BetMGM still posts a full market: Brewers -145 / Orioles +118, run line -1.5 +120 / +1.5 -145, total 8.5 (-105/-115). Away starter May (MIL, R) 4.49/1.29/8.65. Compare to SEA@COL where BOTH market and starter are missing, and to PHI@NYM where one side is missing but market still posted.\n\n[L104] **WHY IT CAUGHT MY ATTENTION:** Illustrates the data-floor problem from the instructions. Market is pricing a game with 50% of starting pitching information unavailable. This is the exact scenario the kill-switch / data-availability rule was designed for (France AH incident reference) — consensus or line availability does not create data.\n\n[L106] **POTENTIAL THESIS:** Market's -145 Brewers / 8.5 total implies expectation of a below-average Orioles starter (or bullpen game) — effectively pricing Milwaukee as if May's mediocre 4.49 ERA is still better than whatever Baltimore throws. Could be exploitable if actual starter announcement is better than market's implicit assumption.\n\n[L108] **STRONGEST COUNTER-THESIS:** No thesis is credible with half the pitching data missing. Any price is a placeholder, not an edge. The line could move violently on announcement, and -145 could become + or -200. Betting into NOT_SET is betting on uncertainty itself, which the evidence boundary says to treat as uncertainty, not an edge. PASS is the correct baseline until starter is confirmed.\n\n[L110] **BIGGEST UNKNOWN:** Who starts for Baltimore, their handedness, and their true level. Without that, ERA/WHIP/K9 comparison is impossible. Also unknown: why BetMGM feels confident to price this but withholds SEA@COL entirely — inconsistency in data handling across games.\n\n[L112] **WHAT I WOULD WANT NEXT:** Official probable starter announcement for Baltimore (name, throws, ERA/WHIP/K9, status). Rules state to treat NOT_SET as uncertainty — so next data is simply confirmation of starter. Until then, no grading. Would also want to know market status: is -145 the opener or has it already moved on news?\n\n[L114] **CLASSIFICATION:** NEEDS DATA\n\n---\n\n[L118] ### NOT SELECTED BUT NOTED\n\n[L120] - **Athletics @ Cleveland (Barnett 7.08/1.62/8.41 vs Espino 2.92/1.22/15.32, CLE -200):** Extreme K9 15.32 highest on slate. Market -200 aligns with ERA gap. Not selected because thesis is too straightforward (ERA says blowout, market says blowout) — less to investigate than games where market contradicts ERA. Total 8.5 even (-110/-110) with a 7.08 ERA starter is itself curious but excluded for cap of five.\n[L121] - **Seattle @ Colorado (Woo 3.88/1.05/9.04 vs NOT_SET, BETMGM_NOT_AVAILABLE):** Only game with no market at all. Pure NEEDS DATA / PASS. Not selected over MIL@BAL because MIL@BAL shows the more dangerous pattern (market pricing without data) while SEA@COL correctly withholds price — less to learn from a withheld market.\n[L122] - **Philadelphia @ New York Mets (NOT_SET vs Thornton 3.44/1.16/6.75, NYM -120):** Second NOT_SET game with a posted line. Similar NEEDS DATA logic to MIL@BAL but away-team missing rather than home; line is tighter (pick'em +100/-120) implying less assumption. Would be #6 if limit were six.\n[L123] - **San Francisco @ Los Angeles Dodgers (Perdomo 3.68/1.36/6.75 vs Glasnow 3.50/0.97/11.24, LAD -300):** Largest moneyline favorite on slate (-300) with not the largest starter gap — suggests market pricing heavily on team beyond starter stats alone, but with no team evidence allowed, cannot evaluate. PASS per evidence boundary.\n[L124] - **Miami @ San Diego (Phillips 3.55/1.33/7.26 vs Pivetta 3.60/1.00/11.16, SD -200):** Nearly identical ERAs but -200 on WHIP/K9 gap. Similar to ATH@CLE but less extreme. Market chooses whiff/WHIP dominance.\n\n---\n\n[L128] ### TOP 3 MOST INTERESTING GAMES:\n\n[L130] 1. **Kansas City Royals @ Pittsburgh Pirates** — ERA-vs-price inversion strongest on slate; market bets on K/WHIP over ERA.\n[L131] 2. **Toronto Blue Jays @ Texas Rangers** — Largest ERA/WHIP/K9 gap prices shortest favorite; market skepticism invites investigation.\n[L132] 3. **New York Yankees @ Arizona Diamondbacks** — ERA leader is home dog (+110) vs WHIP/K9 leader favorite; handedness split isolates peripherals-vs-results debate.\n\n[L134] ### MOST IMPORTANT UNKNOWN ACROSS THE SLATE:\n\n[L136] **Sample size (innings pitched / batters faced) behind every ERA/WHIP/K9 line, plus the three NOT_SET starters and the one withheld market.** Without innings, a 2.38 ERA or 15.32 K9 could be 30 innings or 170 innings — the file gives no way to distinguish stable performance from small-sample spike. Combined with 3 of 15 games missing a starter and 1 of 15 missing a market entirely, the slate fails the data-floor test for any cross-game consensus. The market itself is inconsistent: pricing MIL@BAL and PHI@NYM despite NOT_SET, but pulling SEA@COL entirely.\n\n[L138] ### ONE THING I NOTICED THAT ANOTHER ANALYST MAY MISS:\n\n[L140] **The total pricing is oddly disconnected from starter quality, and totals cluster while starter quality does not.** 11 of 14 priced games sit at 8 or 8.5 regardless of whether starters are 2.38 or 7.08 ERA; only three games are at 7.5 (BOS@TB, TOR@TEX, WSH@STL, MIA@SD actually four) — and TOR@TEX gets 7.5 despite a 4.54 ERA starter, while CHC@CIN gets 8.5 (-120 over) despite two sub-2.85 ERA / 1.15 WHIP starters. The market is not linearly mapping ERA to totals within this slate, which under the evidence boundary warns against treating ERA alone as sufficient. Also: the only left-on-left matchup (BOS@TB: Suarez L vs Seymour L) has one of the low totals (7.5) despite combined ERA ~3.80, a handedness detail that is trivial in isolation but is the only place throws align.\n\n---\n\n[L144] ## SELF-IDENTIFICATION / PROVENANCE\n\n[L146] I certify that this analysis was produced independently from the supplied\n[L147] evidence package and that I did not read another analyst's output before\n[L148] completing this run.\n\n[L150] MODEL / RUNTIME: muse-spark-1.2-contributor-free\n[L151] TIMESTAMP: 2026-09-18T01:50:55Z\n[L152] OUTPUT FILE: C:\\Users\\Dekan AI Brother\\Desktop\\Black Pearl EC\\OpenCode Free Test Run\\BP_T01_muse-spark-1-2-contributor-free_2026-09-18.md\n", "text_chars": 16942, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": ["BLACK_PEARL_TRIAL01_SPARSE_SLATE_2026-09-18.json", "C:\\Users\\Dekan", "Run\\BP_T01_muse-spark-1-2-contributor-free_2026-09-18.md"], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "empty_text_parts": 0, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "5106788a-a49c-4bbf-827d-8682002f9791", "role": "tool", "role_name": "api_tool", "created_at": 1789721566.433498, "updated_at": null, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "text", "part_kinds": ["text"], "text": "The files uploaded in the current message have been fully loaded, so searching them will not provide additional information. However, some previously uploaded files were only partially loaded. If those files are relevant and the visible content is insufficient, you MUST use `files.search` to search their full content before 