ts.\n\n---\n\n[L24] ## GAME-BY-GAME INITIAL READS\n\n[L26] ### GAME 1: Chicago Cubs @ Cincinnati Reds\n\n[L28] **INITIAL READ:** Holmes (2.85/1.15/6.97) vs Burns (2.80/1.15/10.76). Comparable ERA and WHIP. Burns has dramatically higher K/9 (10.76 vs 6.97). Cubs favored -135 on the road. Total 8.5.\n\n[L30] **WHY IT CAUGHT MY ATTENTION:** The K/9 disparity is the largest between two comparable-ERA starters on the slate. Burns' 10.76 K/9 suggests he misses more bats than Holmes, yet the market prices Cincinnati as the underdog. This gap between starter quality signal and market price is worth probing.\n\n[L32] **POTENTIAL THESIS:** Market is overweighting Cubs brand/lineup or Holmes' track record, undervaluing Burns' strikeout dominance. If Burns' K rate translates to run suppression, Reds +110 could have value.\n\n[L34] **STRONGEST COUNTER-THESIS:** K/9 alone does not determine outcomes — Holmes' lower WHIP components and potential ground-ball profile may neutralize Cincinnati's park advantage. Cubs may simply be the better team overall.\n\n[L36] **BIGGEST UNKNOWN:** Team-level evidence (lineups, bullpen, rest) is entirely excluded. The market price likely reflects information I don't have.\n\n[L38] **WHAT I WOULD WANT NEXT:** Bullpen quality for both teams. Lineup OPS vs RHP. Historical starter-vs-lineup data. Park factors for GABP.\n\n[L40] **CLASSIFICATION:** INTEREST\n\n---\n\n[L44] ### GAME 2: Kansas City Royals @ Pittsburgh Pirates\n\n[L46] **INITIAL READ:** Dobnak (2.73/1.27/6.10) vs Skenes (3.78/1.13/10.28). Pirates -190 at home. Total 8.\n\n[L48] **WHY IT CAUGHT MY ATTENTION:** Skenes is the name-brand arm with elite K/9, but his ERA is a full run higher than Dobnak's. The -190 price on Skenes despite the ERA gap is aggressive. Dobnak's low K/9 (6.10) is a yellow flag — he may be surviving on contact management that could regress.\n\n[L50] **POTENTIAL THESIS:** Market correctly prices Skenes as the superior pitcher despite ERA — the K/9 and WHIP suggest Sken