** Barnett (7.08/1.62/8.41) vs Espino (2.92/1.22/15.32). Guardians -200 at home. Total 8.5.\n\n[L84] **WHY IT CAUGHT MY ATTENTION:** Espino's 15.32 K/9 is the highest on the slate by a wide margin. Combined with a 2.92 ERA, this is an elite profile. Barnett's 7.08 ERA is the worst on the slate. The -200 price feels justified by the starter mismatch alone.\n\n[L86] **POTENTIAL THESIS:** Cleveland run line -1.5 (+110) is the play. Espino's K dominance against a starter with a 7.08 ERA and 1.62 WHIP should create a blowout scenario.\n\n[L88] **STRONGEST COUNTER-THESIS:** -200 implies ~67% win probability. The Guardians' lineup and bullpen must perform to justify that price. Also, high-K pitchers can be volatile — one bad inning with runners on base erases the K advantage.\n\n[L90] **BIGGEST UNKNOWN:** Barnett's sample size. A 7.08 ERA could be 3 bad starts or 20. If it's a small sample, Barnett may be better than the number suggests. Also: Guardians lineup vs. RHP.\n\n[L92] **WHAT I WOULD WANT NEXT:** Barnett's innings pitched. Whether Cleveland is in a playoff race (motivation factor). Espino's walk rate (high K pitchers sometimes walk too many).\n\n[L94] **CLASSIFICATION:** INTEREST\n\n---\n\n[L98] ### GAME 5: Boston Red Sox @ Tampa Bay Rays\n\n[L100] **INITIAL READ:** Suarez (3.33/1.19/8.50 L) vs Seymour (4.27/1.14/11.04 L). Both lefties. Rays -130 at home. Total 7.5.\n\n[L102] **WHY IT CAUGHT MY ATTENTION:** Suarez has the better ERA (3.33 vs 4.27), yet Boston is the underdog. The market is pricing Tampa Bay as the better team despite the starter ERA gap. The total is the second-lowest on the slate (7.5), suggesting a pitcher-friendly game.\n\n[L104] **POTENTIAL THESIS:** Rays are correctly priced — their lineup may be significantly better than Boston's against left-handed pitching. Seymour's 11.04 K/9 suggests he has swing-and-miss stuff that the ERA doesn't capture.\n\n[L106] **STRONGEST COUNTER-THESIS:** Suarez's 3.33 ERA is backed by a strong 1.19 WHIP. If Bost