oth the moneyline and run-line pricing point toward St. Louis. That makes the game a strong candidate for investigating what information is missing from the slate.\n\n[L95] POTENTIAL THESIS:\n[L96] If the listed starter metrics are the relevant information and remain current, Washington could be underpriced on the moneyline or on the -1.5 run line. The 7.5 total is also worth separating from the side market.\n\n[L98] STRONGEST COUNTER-THESIS:\n[L99] The starter differences are modest rather than decisive, and season ERA/WHIP/K9 may not capture the current matchup. The market may contain information absent from the JSON, including information that reverses the starter-only reading.\n\n[L101] BIGGEST UNKNOWN:\n[L102] Why the market favors St. Louis when every supplied starter metric favors Washington, and which omitted factors, if any, explain that difference.\n\n[L104] WHAT I WOULD WANT NEXT:\n[L105] Confirm both probable starters and obtain the missing lineup, bullpen, and other allowed contextual inputs. Reprice the side and run line before treating the starter-only discrepancy as an edge.\n\n[L107] CLASSIFICATION:\nWATCH\n\n[L110] ### 4. Milwaukee Brewers @ Baltimore Orioles — 4:05 PM PT — Oriole Park at Camden Yards\n\nGAME:\n[L113] Milwaukee Brewers @ Baltimore Orioles 2026-09-18\n\n[L115] INITIAL READ:\n[L116] Milwaukee is priced -145 with Dustin May listed at 4.49 ERA, 1.29 WHIP, and 8.65 K/9. Baltimore's starter is `NOT_SET`, so a complete pitching comparison is impossible. The market assigns Milwaukee a 59.2% raw implied probability even though one side's starter information is missing.\n\n[L118] WHY IT CAUGHT MY ATTENTION:\n[L119] The price is available and directional, but the evidence is incomplete. Milwaukee is -1.5 at +120, the total is 8.5 with Over -105 and Under -115, and there is no Baltimore starter ERA, WHIP, or K/9 against which to test those prices.\n\n[L121] POTENTIAL THESIS:\n[L122] If Baltimore's eventual starter is weaker than May on the supplied measures, or if the market's missing-starter assumption is unfavorable to Baltimore, the Brewers side could merit further investigation. The conclusion is entirely conditional on the missing starter information.\n\n[L124] STRONGEST COUNTER-THESIS:\n[L125] The absence of Baltimore's starter prevents a fair comparison. The -145 price could reflect information not present in the JSON, or the market could be mispriced; the supplied evidence cannot distinguish those possibilities.\n\n[L127] BIGGEST UNKNOWN:\n[L128] Baltimore's starter identity, status, and listed season pitching statistics.\n\n[L130] WHAT I WOULD WANT NEXT:\n[L131] Obtain Baltimore's starter information and confirm both probable statuses, then reassess the moneyline, run line, and total with a complete starter comparison.\n\n[L133] CLASSIFICATION:\n[L134] NEEDS DATA\n\n[L136] ### 5. Philadelphia Phillies @ New York Mets — 4:15 PM PT — Citi Field\n\nGAME:\n[L139] Philadelphia Phillies @ New York Mets 2026-09-18\n\n[L141] INITIAL READ:\n[L142] Philadelphia's starter is `NOT_SET`, while New York's Zac Thornton is listed at 3.44 ERA, 1.16 WHIP, and 6.75 K/9. BetMGM prices the Mets -120 and Phillies +100, giving New York a 54.5% raw implied probability despite the missing Phillies starter.\n\n[L144] WHY IT CAUGHT MY ATTENTION:\n[L145] The moneyline is directional, but the pitching evidence is incomplete. The run line is Phillies -1.5 at +165 and Mets +1.5 at -200, while the total is 8.5 with Over -105 and Under -115. The side price and run-line price answer different questions and should not be treated as confirmation of one another.\n\n[L147] POTENTIAL THESIS:\n[L148] If the missing Phillies starter proves materially stronger than the available information suggests, Philadelphia's +100 moneyline or -1.5 at +165 could deserve investigation. Without that information, the thesis remains speculative.\n\n[L150] STRONGEST COUNTER-THESIS:\n[L151] There is no supplied Phillies starter baseline, so no probabili