ullpen perfection. Secondary lean: the even -110 total tilts over if Barnett's WHIP is the trusted number.\n[L44] **STRONGEST COUNTER-THESIS:** 15.32 K/9 is an extreme outlier versus the rest of the slate (next best: 12.77) and pattern-matches a short sample. If that line regresses, -200 on any team while Barnett can keep the game within one swing is a bad price — and the unjuiced 8.5 total may be the market signaling it does not fully believe either line.\n[L45] **BIGGEST UNKNOWN:** Espino's evidence vintage — stabilized full-season performance or small-sample artifact? Secondary: Cleveland's bullpen state (excluded from evidence), which decides whether -1.5 survives innings 6–9.\n[L46] **WHAT I WOULD WANT NEXT:** Espino's innings per start and recent game log; Barnett's home-run rate; both bullpens' last-two-weeks usage and runs allowed; lineup context.\n[L47] **CLASSIFICATION:** INTEREST\n\n---\n\n[L51] ### GAME 2: New York Yankees @ Arizona Diamondbacks — 6:40 PM PT, Chase Field\n\n[L53] **GAME:** New York Yankees @ Arizona Diamondbacks, 6:40 PM PT, Chase Field\n[L54] **INITIAL READ:** Eduardo Rodriguez (2.62 ERA / 1.22 WHIP / 6.81 K/9) vs Gerrit Cole (3.67 / 1.13 / 9.13). The home starter owns the better ERA by more than a full run; the road starter owns only a modest WHIP/K edge (+0.09 WHIP, +2.3 K/9). Yankees are -135 on the road anyway.\n[L55] **WHY IT CAUGHT MY ATTENTION:** Across this slate, when ERA and peripherals conflict, BetMGM pays for peripherals — Pirates -190 over Dobnak's better ERA, White Sox -135 over Sears' better ERA, Padres -200 despite Pivetta's ERA (3.60) edging higher than Phillips' (3.55). This game breaks that rule. A slate-internal convention with an exception is a stronger signal than any single stat line.\n[L56] **POTENTIAL THESIS:** Diamondbacks +110. If the slate's own convention (peripherals drive price) is applied consistently, this game should be near pick'em, making Arizona the largest starter-vs-price mismatch outside Cleveland.\n[L57] **STRONGEST COUNTER-THESIS:** The market may be pricing team quality and lineup depth that are invisible inside the permitted-evidence boundary, and ERA alone is explicitly insufficient evidence. Cole's slight peripheral edge could be the honest signal, and a 6.81 K/9 ball-in-play-heavy lefty may be exactly the profile the market is targeting.\n[L58] **BIGGEST UNKNOWN:** Team-level offense quality for both sides (excluded), and whether Rodriguez's 2.62 is stabilized or sample-driven.\n[L59] **WHAT I WOULD WANT NEXT:** Team offense metrics split L/R; Rodriguez's game log; home/road splits for both starters.\n[L60] **CLASSIFICATION:** INTEREST\n\n---\n\n[L64] ### GAME 3: Chicago Cubs @ Cincinnati Reds — 3:40 PM PT, Great American Ball Park\n\n[L66] **GAME:** Chicago Cubs @ Cincinnati Reds, 3:40 PM PT, Great American Ball Park\n[L67] **INITIAL READ:** Chase Burns (2.80 ERA / 1.15 WHIP / 10.76 K/9) vs Clay Holmes (2.85 / 1.15 / 6.97). Near-identical ERA and WHIP; Burns carries a +3.8 K/9 edge. Cubs -135 on the road.\n[L68] **WHY IT CAUGHT MY ATTENTION:** The starter surface is a coin flip with a clear strikeout advantage to the home pitcher, yet the road team is -135. Given this slate's demonstrated peripherals-driven pricing, the Reds should sit much closer to even; +110 stands out as the second rule-break alongside the Yankees game. Also notable: with two sub-2.9 ERA starters, the OVER (8.5, -120) is the juiced side — the market expects runs anyway.\n[L69] **POTENTIAL THESIS:** Reds +110 moneyline — starter parity plus K edge at a plus price. Secondary: Under 8.5 (+100) if the starter ERAs are the truth and the over juice is bullpen-driven.\n[L70] **STRONGEST COUNTER-THESIS:** The market may know Holmes' low-K profile is backed by elite run prevention and a materially superior Cubs team (none of which is in evidence), while Burns' K rate may carry command risk that a small-sample WHIP understates. Cubs team quality alone could justify -135.\n[L71] **BIGGEST 