ress sharply. Barnett's 8.41 K9 suggests he misses bats too — he may not be as bad as the ERA indicates, meaning a high-K duel where variance spikes.\n\n[L52] **BIGGEST UNKNOWN:** Espino's sample size / innings ceiling. Is 15.32 K9 over 30 IP or 150 IP? That changes everything. Also: bullpen availability behind both starters is excluded.\n\n[L54] **WHAT I WOULD WANT NEXT:** Espino's IP total and games started this season. Barnett's xFIP/SIERA vs ERA to see if he's been unlucky. Bullpen ERAs for both teams. Athletics lineup vs high-K RHP.\n\n[L56] **CLASSIFICATION:** INTEREST\n\n---\n\n[L60] ### GAME 2: Kansas City Royals @ Pittsburgh Pirates\n\n[L62] **GAME:** Kansas City Royals @ Pittsburgh Pirates, 3:40 PM PT, PNC Park\n\n[L64] **INITIAL READ:** Inverted starter profiles. Dobnak (2.73 ERA, 1.27 WHIP, 6.1 K9) vs Skenes (3.78 ERA, 1.13 WHIP, 10.28 K9). Pirates -190, Royals +155. Total 8.\n\n[L66] **WHY IT CAUGHT MY ATTENTION:** The ERA/WHIP relationship is flipped. Dobnak has the better ERA but worse WHIP and much worse K9. Skenes has the worse ERA but better WHIP and far superior K9. This is a classic BABIP-dependence signal: Dobnak's 2.73 ERA with a 1.27 WHIP and 6.1 K9 suggests he's living on batted-ball luck that typically regresses. Skenes' 3.78 ERA with a 1.13 WHIP and 10.28 K9 suggests he may be running unlucky (low WHIP + high K9 but elevated ERA = possible HR or sequencing issues). The market has Pittsburgh at -190, which is a heavy price for a starter with a 3.78 ERA.\n\n[L68] **POTENTIAL THESIS:** Dobnak is a regression candidate. His ERA should rise. The Royals at +155 may have value if Dobnak's true talent is closer to his WHIP/K9 than his ERA. The pricing may be anchored to ERA surface numbers.\n\n[L70] **STRONGEST COUNTER-THESIS:** Skenes is the kind of pitcher who can dominate despite ERA — high K9 pitchers have higher variance per start but also higher peak innings. A bad defensive inning or a HR can inflate ERA without meaning the pitcher is bad. A