d Skenes, and explicitly says that the conflict cannot be resolved from this dataset. That is good methodology behavior. fileciteturn33file0L26-L40\n\nSame with Milwaukee–Baltimore. Instead of inventing the missing Baltimore starter, it asks a more interesting methodological question: *can the market price itself tell us anything when the underlying player evidence is missing?* Then it stops short of trusting it and leaves the game at WATCH. fileciteturn33file0L80-L94\n\nThat is very Kimi.\n\nBut he does have a meaningful Black Pearl strike.\n\nHis closing run-line thesis gets too clever.\n\nHe says PIT -190 with PIT -1.5 +115 means the market is simultaneously saying Pittsburgh will likely win but **“likely wins by exactly one.”** That is not what those prices establish. A team can have a high straight-up win probability while having a much lower probability of winning by 2+. Plus money on -1.5 is completely compatible with that; it does not specifically imply a one-run win. fileciteturn33file0L141-L143\n\nAnd then he generalizes it into a slate-wide “narrow-margin favorites” pattern because every -190-or-heavier favorite has a plus-money run line. That is an interesting observation, but the interpretation outruns the evidence.\n\nThere are a couple of smaller Kimi tells too. On Cleveland he says Espino’s 15.32 K9 is “more likely” a small sample and even offers “call-up, injury return” as examples. Those are not in the packet. fileciteturn33file0L44-L58 And later he calls rate-stat/peripheral divergence a “classic signature of small samples,” which is a baseball prior, not something established by the sparse JSON. fileciteturn33file0L137-L139\n\nSo my card is:\n\n| Dimension | Kimi K3-256 Low |\n|---|---|\n| Structural reasoning | **Excellent** |\n| Grounding | **Strong-Mixed** |\n| Methodology thinking | **Excellent** |\n| Uncertainty handling | **Strong** |\n| Novelty | **Excellent** |\n| Gap filling | **Moderate-Low** |\n| Market interpretation | **Good, one significant RL overreach** |\n| First-pass Black Pearl berth | **Not my first choice** |\n| Methodology/challenger berth | **Absolutely** |\n\nAnd that lines up almost perfectly with how you already use him.\n\n**Nex Pro** says: “Show me what the evidence actually supports.”\n\n**Grok** says: “These two things contradict each other.”\n\n**Kimi** says: “What does the relationship between these evidence structures mean, and what methodology should we use to investigate it?”\n\nThat third job is incredibly valuable — it just shouldn’t be allowed to quietly become the first job.\n\nAlso: considering this was **low thinking**, Kimi did plenty. I would not spend more compute trying to turn him into Nex. That’s not his lane.", "text_chars": 3215, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "empty_text_parts": 0, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "cd6b036b-a4b1-4064-8ed3-2635f7d66774", "role": "tool", "role_name": "api_tool", "created_at": 1789724139.5367362, "updated_at": null, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "multimodal_text", "part_kinds": ["text", "text"], "text": "Make sure to include fileciteturn33file0L5-L8 in your response to cite this file, or to surface it as a link. Replace the placeholder line range with the actual line range from the result that best fits the snippet you want to surface with the citation. If you cite multiple files, use a separate complete `filecite` marker for each file. Never put multiple `turnNfileM` targets inside a single `filecite` marker.\nUse ref_id \"turn33file0\" to reference this file. For broad questions or unknown locations, retrieve relevant chunks with `files.search` scoped to this file. Use `files.find` only for exact text, and use `files.read` for a known range or to expand relevant results. Full file size: 156 file lines.\n[L1] # BLACK PEARL PROVING GROUNDS — TRIAL 01\n\n[L3] MODEL / RUNTIME: Kimi Code CLI\n[L4] PROVIDER: Moonshot AI\n[L5] VERSION / MODEL ID: UNKNOWN (not exposed by the runtime)\n[L6] ROLE: Independent Blind Analyst\n[L7] RUN TIMESTAMP: 2026-09-18T09:33:39Z\n[L8] TRIAL ID: BP-T01\n[L9] SOURCE DATASET: BLACK_PEARL_TRIAL01_SPARSE_SLATE_2026-09-18.json\n[L10] ANALYSIS MODE: BLIND\n[L11] OUTCOME DATA ACCESSED: NO\n[L12] OTHER ANALYST OUTPUT ACCESSED: NO\n\n---\n\n[L16] ## SLATE REVIEW\n\n[L18] 15 games supplied. Evidence per game is limited to matchup, time, venue, probable starter (name/status/handedness, season ERA/WHIP/K9) and BetMGM moneyline/run line/total. Three games carry structural holes: Milwaukee@Baltimore (home starter NOT_SET), Philadelphia@NY Mets (away starter NOT_SET), and Seattle@Colorado (home starter NOT_SET **and** all BetMGM markets NOT_AVAILABLE). Seattle@Colorado has no usable market evidence at all — it is a pure PASS.\n\n[L20] All analysis below uses only the supplied numbers. ERA alone is not treated as sufficient evidence; where ERA and WHIP/K9 diverge, the divergence itself is the signal.\n\n---\n\n[L24] ## SELECTED GAMES (5)\n\n[L26] ### GAME 1: Kansas City Royals @ Pittsburgh Pirates — 3:40 PM PT, PNC Park\n\n[L28] INITIAL READ: Market prices Pittsburgh as a heavy home favorite (-190) despite the Royals' starter holding the better ERA (Dobnak 2.73 vs Skenes 3.78).\n\n[L30] WHY IT CAUGHT MY ATTENTION: This is the clearest internal contradiction on the slate. The away starter's ERA beats the home starter's by a full run, yet the moneyline implies roughly a 65% home win probability. The run line structure (KC +1.5 at -140) says the market expects this to be close even while pricing a lopsided winner — a \"favorite wins but barely\" shape.\n\n[L32] POTENTIAL THESIS: Skenes' WHIP (1.13) and K9 (10.28) are meaningfully better than Dobnak's (1.27 / 6.1), so the ERA gap may overstate Dobnak's actual performance level; the market may be pricing the peripherals, not the ERA. If so, the price is coherent and Dobnak's ERA is the misleading number.\n\n[L34] STRONGEST COUNTER-THESIS: If Dobnak's ERA reflects something the peripherals miss (weak contact, sequencing, defense), then +155 on the team with the lower-ERA starter is underpriced. ERA-vs-peripherals divergence cannot be resolved from this dataset.\n\n[L36] BIGGEST UNKNOWN: Why Dobnak's ERA (2.73) is so far ahead of his WHIP/K9 profile — luck, defense, or genuine skill. No bullpen, lineup, or team-form data exists to arbitrate.\n\n[L38] WHAT I WOULD WANT NEXT: Starter innings pitched / sample size for both, team records or run differential, bullpen quality, and at least one other sportsbook price to test whether BetMGM's -190 is an outlier.\n\n[L40] CLASSIFICATION: INTEREST\n\n---\n\n[L44] ### GAME 2: Athletics @ Cleveland Guardians — 4:10 PM PT, Progressive Field\n\n[L46] INITIAL READ: Largest starter-quality gap on the slate: Barnett (7.08 ERA / 1.62 WHIP) vs Espino (2.92 ERA / 1.22 WHIP / 15.32 K9).\n\n[L48] WHY IT CAUGHT MY ATTENTION: Two things. First, Espino's K9 of 15.32 is a wild outlier versus every other starter on the slate (next highest is 12.77) — that profile is either dominant or a tiny sample. Second, despite the enormous starter gap, Cleveland is only -200 with the total sitting at a flat 8.5 (-110/-110). For a 7.08-ERA starter, the market is pricing this as a comfortable-but-not-blowout home win, and the symmetric total pricing suggests no strong scoring lean either way.\n\n[L50] POTENTIAL THESIS: The edge, if any, is in the derivative markets rather than the moneyline: if Espino is genuinely elite, Cleveland -1.5 at +110 is more interesting than -200 on the moneyline. The plus-money run line is the market inviting a decision.\n\n[L52] STRONGEST COUNTER-THESIS: A 15.32 K9 over a full season is nearly unheard of; the more likely reading is a small sample (call-up, injury return), which makes Espino's 2.92 ERA unreliable too. Betting either side of a small-sample pitcher against a genuinely bad one is still betting on noise. Also, nothing here prices the Athletics' offense — bad starter ≠ automatic cover.\n\n[L54] BIGGEST UNKNOWN: Espino's sample size. Everything about this game's pricing interpretation hinges on whether 15.32 K9 is real or 30 innings of noise.\n\n[L56] WHAT I WOULD WANT NEXT: Innings/starts for Espino and Barnett, and team run-scoring context to know whether Cleveland can actually cover -1.5.\n\n[L58] CLASSIFICATION: NEEDS DATA\n\n---\n\n[L62] ### GAME 3: Toronto Blue Jays @ Texas Rangers — 5:05 PM PT, Globe Life Field\n\n[L64] INITIAL READ: Toronto is -140 on the road with a starter (Cease: 2.38 ERA / 1.06 WHIP / 12.77 K9) who is better than the home starter (Rocker: 4.54 / 1.43 / 8.57) on every supplied metric.\n\n[L66] WHY IT CAUGHT MY ATTENTION: This is the slate's cleanest starter mismatch in the favorite's direction — and the total is only 7.5 with juice on the over (-118). A low total with a dominant away starter is a coherent \"Toronto wins a low-scoring game\" picture, but -140 on the road is a real price, and road favorites are where sparse datasets hide traps.\n\n[L68] POTENTIAL THESIS: Cease's full-line dominance (best ERA on the slate, second-best K9, best WHIP among starters with complete data except Glasnow's 0.97) justifies the road favoritism; the game is priced honestly and the honest answer is Toronto. If anything the under at -102 (Rocker's ERA aside, a 7.5 total with one ace) is the secondary angle.\n\n[L70] STRONGEST COUNTER-THESIS: The total is 7.5 with over juice — the market expects Rocker to give up runs, meaning Toronto's offense is expected to do the work, not Cease. If Toronto's bats underperform, -140 buys a pitcher whose dominance only covers half the game. Without lineup or bullpen data, \"best starter\" is half an argument.\n\n[L72] BIGGEST UNKNOWN: Whether Toronto's price reflects team strength the dataset can't show, or whether the market is simply paying for Cease — the distinction