 this file. For broad questions or unknown locations, retrieve relevant chunks with `files.search` scoped to this file. Use `files.find` only for exact text, and use `files.read` for a known range or to expand relevant results. Full file size: 106 file lines.\n[L1] # BLACK PEARL PROVING GROUNDS — TRIAL 01\n\n[L3] MODEL / RUNTIME: Kimi Code CLI\n[L4] PROVIDER: UNKNOWN (not exposed in runtime-visible information)\n[L5] VERSION / MODEL ID: UNKNOWN (not exposed in runtime-visible information)\n[L6] ROLE: Independent Blind Analyst\n[L7] RUN TIMESTAMP: 2026-09-18 02:36 PDT\n[L8] TRIAL ID: BP-T01\n[L9] SOURCE DATASET: BLACK_PEARL_TRIAL01_SPARSE_SLATE_2026-09-18.json\n[L10] ANALYSIS MODE: BLIND\n[L11] OUTCOME DATA ACCESSED: NO\n[L12] OTHER ANALYST OUTPUT ACCESSED: NO\n\n---\n\n[L16] ## Slate scan\n\n[L18] 15 games. Three have missing starters (MIL@BAL home, PHI@NYM away, SEA@COL home) and one (SEA@COL) has no BetMGM market at all. Those are data-floor problems, not edges. My attention goes to games where the sparse evidence itself creates a question.\n\n---\n\n[L22] ## Selected games (≤5)\n\n[L24] ### 1. Athletics @ Cleveland Guardians — 4:10 PM PT, Progressive Field\n\n[L26] GAME: Athletics@Cleveland Guardians 2026-09-18\n[L27] INITIAL READ: Guardians heavily favored (-200), and the sparse line supports it — but the size of the gap between starters is the story.\n[L28] WHY IT CAUGHT MY ATTENTION: Daniel Espino: 2.92 ERA, 1.22 WHIP, 15.32 K/9. Mason Barnett: 7.08 ERA, 1.62 WHIP. That K/9 gap (15.32 vs 8.41) plus a 4+ ERA gap is the largest pitching mismatch on the slate, yet Cleveland is only -200, cheaper than Skenes' Pittsburgh (-190 is close, actually) and the Dodgers (-300).\n[L29] POTENTIAL THESIS: Market is pricing some of the gap but maybe not all of it; a 15+ K/9 arm facing a 7 ERA arm at home should win ~70-75% of the time, and -200 implies 66.7%.\n[L30] STRONGEST COUNTER-THESIS: ERA alone is not evidence (rule stated explicitly). Barnett's 7.08 could be small-sample noise, and Espino's K/9 could come with walk/command issues the WHIP (1.22, vs his ERA) partially confirms. No team/bullpen/lineup data allowed — the -200 may already fully capture it.\n[L31] BIGGEST UNKNOWN: Whether Espino's strikeout dominance translates to run suppression against this lineup (lineups excluded by design).\n[L32] WHAT I WOULD WANT NEXT: Bullpen status, both lineups, park/umpire context, and Barnett's recent form vs season aggregate.\n[L33] CLASSIFICATION: INTEREST\n\n[L35] ### 2. Kansas City Royals @ Pittsburgh Pirates — 3:40 PM PT, PNC Park\n\n[L37] GAME: Kansas City Royals@Pittsburgh Pirates 2026-09-18\n[L38] INITIAL READ: A market contradiction worth flagging: Pittsburgh is -190 with Paul Skenes on 3.78 ERA / 1.13 WHIP, while Randy Dobnak carries a better ERA (2.73) for the underdog.\n[L39] WHY IT CAUGHT MY ATTENTION: This is the one game where the favorite's starter has a markedly WORSE ERA than the dog's starter (3.78 vs 2.73), yet the market is near the slate's most expensive favorite. The market is clearly weighing something beyond season ERA — reputation, underlying stuff, team quality — none of which I'm allowed to use.\n[L40] POTENTIAL THESIS: If season ERA/WHIP/K9 is all the evidence I'm permitted, KC +155 with the better-season pitcher is a defensible value position on the sparse data alone. WHIP nearly equal (1.27 vs 1.13 favors Skenes), K/9 favors Skenes heavily (10.28 vs 6.10) — so maybe not.\n[L41] STRONGEST COUNTER-THESIS: K/9 and WHIP both favor Skenes; ERA is the noisiest of the three allowed stats. The market may be right and ERA-mineable value is an illusion. Also Dobnak's 1.27 WHIP with 2.73 ERA suggests sequencing luck.\n[L42] BIGGEST UNKNOWN: Skenes' ERA being 3.78 — is that a recent slump baked into a season number, or steady state? (Recent-form data not provided.)\n[L43] WHAT I WOULD WANT NEXT: Split stats, Skenes' last 5 starts, and why the market prices him like an ace despite the 3.78.\n[L44] CLASSIFICATION: WATCH\n\n[L46] ### 3. Washington Nationals @ St. Louis Cardinals — 5:15 PM PT, Busch Stadium\n\n[L48] GAME: Washington Nationals@St. Louis Cardinals 2026-09-18\n[L49] INITIAL READ: Near pick'em (-105/-115) between two starters with nearly identical season lines: Cavalli 3.12/1.17/10.6 vs Leahy 3.33/1.31/8.53.\n[L50] WHY IT CAUGHT MY ATTENTION: It's the tightest market on the slate (total also 7.5, the lowest available line alongside two others) and the sparse stats slightly favor the AWAY arm on ERA, WHIP, and K/9 — yet the away team is the cheap side. Small edges in all three allowed stats pointing the same direction is rare on this slate.\n[L51] POTENTIAL THESIS: Nationals -105 with the better starter in every allowed metric is the cleanest \"price vs sparse data\" disagreement on the board. If both bullpens/lineups are average, the starter gap (0.21 ERA, 0.14 WHIP, 2.07 K/9) justifies closer to -120.\n[L52] STRONGEST COUNTER-THESIS: Home field is worth roughly the -10 cent flip by itself; the market may be perfectly efficient here and the stat edge within noise.\n[L53] BIGGEST UNKNOWN: Home/away and handedness splits of both starters (platoon data excluded).\n[L54] WHAT I WOULD WANT NEXT: Team offense vs RHP for both clubs, bullpen rest state.\n[L55] CLASSIFICATION: INTEREST\n\n[L57] ### 4. Minnesota Twins @ Los Angeles Angels — 6:38 PM PT, Angel Stadium\n\n[L59] GAME: Minnesota Twins@Los Angeles Angels 2026-09-18\n[L60] INITIAL READ: The two worst pitching lines on the slate face each other: Prielipp 5.24/1.34 and Rodriguez 6.10/1.56 — yet the total is only 8.5.\n[L61] WHY IT CAUGHT MY ATTENTION: Every other game with one 5+ ERA starter got a 9-ish feel (several 8.5s with far better arms). Two bad starters plus the lowest-strikeout profile of the bad-arm games (8.17 K/9 means more balls in play) makes 8.5 look like a candidate misprice on the sparse evidence alone.\n[L62] POTENTIAL THESIS: Over 8.5 at -105. Combined ERA profile suggests a game state where both offenses get 5+ runs more often than the ~52% breakeven implies.\n[L63] STRONGEST COUNTER-THESIS: Totals bake in park, weather, umpire, and bullpen quality — all excluded from my evidence. 8.5 might be the park-adjusted equilibrium; ERA alone is explicitly called out as insufficient in my rules.\n[L64] BIGGEST UNKNOWN: Bullpen quality behind both starters (a bad starter pulled early can lower runs if the pen is good — or raise them if it's bad).\n[L65] WHAT I WOULD WANT NEXT: Bullpen ERA ranks, park factor for Angel Stadium, umpire.\n[L66] CLASSIFICATION: NEEDS DATA\n\n[L68] ### 5. New York Yankees @ Arizona Diamondbacks — 6:40 PM PT, Chase Field\n\n[L70] GAME: New York Yankees@Arizona Diamondbacks 2026-09-18\n[L71] INITIAL READ: Eduardo Rodriguez (2.62 ERA, 6.81 K/9) at home as a +110 dog against Gerrit Cole (3.67 ERA, 9.13 K/9) and the -135 Yankees.\n[L72] WHY IT CAUGHT MY ATTENTION: The better-season ERA belongs to the underdog, and it's a lefty — but I can't use platoon splits. The disagreement between ERA (favors AZ) and K/9 (favors NYY) is the kind of stat-vs-stat split that usually means the market knows which stat to weight.\n[L73] POTENTIAL THESIS: Rodriguez's 2.62 with a 1.22 WHIP and low K/9 is the classic profile of a pitcher living on weak contact — fragile, and the 9.13 K/9 arm is the more stable asset. NYY -135 could still be fair-to-light.\n[L74] STRONGEST COUNTER-THESIS: I have no evidence weak-contact pitching is fragile; that's outside knowledge reasoning on my part. On the allowed stats, AZ's starter simply has the better season.\n[L75] BIGGEST UNKNOWN: Same-handedness lineup quality for both clubs (excluded evidence).\n[L76] WHAT I WOULD WANT NEXT: Both teams' performance vs LHP / RHP, Chase Field park run environment.\n[L77] CLASSIFICATION: WATCH\n\n---\n\n[L81] ## Top 3 most interesting games\n\n[L83] 1. **Athletics @ Guardians** — biggest allowed-stat mismatch on the slate; is -200 enough?\n[L84] 2. **Nationals @ Cardinals** — only game where the away starter wins all three allowed stats yet prices as the dog-side of a pick'em.\n[L85] 3. **Twins @ Angels** — two worst starter profiles, modest 8.5 total; over is the sparse-data lean but unverifiable without excluded evidence.\n\n[L87] ## Most important unknown across the slate\n\n[L89] **Bullpens.** In every game above the starter gap is thin or negative, and with relievers excluded from evidence, the single biggest run-suppression variable for 4-6 innings of each game is invisible. Any thesis built on starters alone has a hidden half.\n\n[L91] ## One thing I noticed that another analyst may miss\n\n[L93] The three NOT_SET starter games (MIL@BAL, PHI@NYM, SEA@COL) are not just \"missing data\" — they create asymmetric market information: Milwaukee (-145) and the Mets (-120) are priced confidently despite one starter being completely unknown on each side. A blind analyst should treat those prices as carrying information they can't see, i.e., the market knows who the probable is even when my JSON doesn't. Fading or following those lines from sparse data alone is guessing — PASS on all three regardless of how tempting the number looks. Also: Seattle@Colorado has NO BetMGM market at all, the only game on the slate so excluded — it's the cleanest PASS available.\n\n---\n\n[L97] ## SELF-IDENTIFICATION / PROVENANCE\n\n[L99] I certify that this analysis was produced independently from the supplied\n[L100] evidence package and that I did not read another analyst's output before\n[L101] completing this run.\n\n[L103] MODEL / RUNTIME: Kimi Code CLI\n[L104] TIMESTAMP: 2026-09-18 02:36 PDT\n[L105] OUTPUT FILE: BP_T01_kimi-code-cli_02_2026-09-18.md\n", "text_chars": 10012, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": ["BLACK_PEARL_TRIAL01_SPARSE_SLATE_2026-09-18.json", "BP_T01_kimi-code-cli_02_2026-09-18.md"], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "empty_text_parts": 0, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "83a0f868-e2d