sed thesis.\nWHAT I WOULD WANT NEXT:\nStarter innings/sample size, pitch count/workload context, bullpen strength and availability, team offense, lineup quality, and broader market pricing.\nCLASSIFICATION:\nINTEREST\n\n## GAME 3\n\nGAME: Miami Marlins @ San Diego Padres 2026-09-18\nINITIAL READ:\nThe moneyline gap is large even though the two starters have nearly identical ERAs; the separation comes primarily from WHIP and strikeout rate.\nWHY IT CAUGHT MY ATTENTION:\nTyler Phillips is listed at 3.55 ERA, 1.33 WHIP, 7.26 K/9. Nick Pivetta is at 3.60 ERA, 1.00 WHIP, 11.16 K/9. San Diego is -200, Miami +165, with San Diego -1.5 at +115 and a 7.5 total.\nPOTENTIAL THESIS:\nThis game is useful because it tests whether the market is weighting underlying starter quality beyond ERA. Pivetta's lower WHIP and much higher K/9 point one way, while the near-equal ERAs make the -200 price look aggressive if starter ERA is viewed in isolation.\nSTRONGEST COUNTER-THESIS:\nThe market may be reflecting team-level advantages entirely absent from the dataset. The price cannot be called excessive merely because the starter ERAs are similar.\nBIGGEST UNKNOWN:\nHow much of the -200 price comes from non-starter team strength versus Pivetta's stronger WHIP/K profile.\nWHAT I WOULD WANT NEXT:\nTeam offense, bullpen, lineups, handedness splits, starter workload, and market consensus.\nCLASSIFICATION:\nWATCH\n\n## GAME 4\n\nGAME: Chicago Cubs @ Cincinnati Reds 2026-09-18\nINITIAL READ:\nThe starters have nearly identical ERA and WHIP, but Chase Burns has a materially higher strikeout rate; despite that, Chicago is the road favorite.\nWHY IT CAUGHT MY ATTENTION:\nClay Holmes is listed at 2.85 ERA, 1.15 WHIP, and 6.97 K/9. Chase Burns is at 2.80 ERA, 1.15 WHIP, and 10.76 K/9. Yet Chicago is -135 and Cincinnati +110. The total is 8.5.\nPOTENTIAL THESIS:\nCincinnati deserves further investigation because the supplied starter data do not obviously explain why Chicago is favored. With ERA and WHIP